00:04:07,890 --> 00:04:12,780
So we can start with presenting
first the attention timeline.

99
00:04:12,780 --> 00:04:17,370
So I will say initially we used
to have this prehistoric era,

100
00:04:17,370 --> 00:04:21,660
where we had very simple
methods, so language,

101
00:04:21,660 --> 00:04:22,407
for example.

102
00:04:22,407 --> 00:04:24,240
So like you had a lot
of rule-based methods,

103
00:04:24,240 --> 00:04:27,270
you had parsing, you
have RNNs, LSTMs.

104
00:04:27,270 --> 00:04:29,280
And that all
changed, I will say,

105
00:04:29,280 --> 00:04:32,760
in the beginning of around
2014 when people started

106
00:04:32,760 --> 00:04:34,530
studying attention mechanisms.

107
00:04:34,530 --> 00:04:37,170
And this was around say
initially around images,

108
00:04:37,170 --> 00:04:39,600
can you adapt the mechanism
of-- how attention

109
00:04:39,600 --> 00:04:40,920
works in the human brain.

110
00:04:40,920 --> 00:04:42,840
Images, can you focus
on different parts,

111
00:04:42,840 --> 00:04:47,670
which might be more salient or
more relevant to a user query

112
00:04:47,670 --> 00:04:48,840
or what you care about.

113
00:04:48,840 --> 00:04:51,540
And I will say
attention exploded

114
00:04:51,540 --> 00:04:55,380
in the beginning of 2017, with
the paper Attention Is All You

115
00:04:55,380 --> 00:04:57,630
Need by Ashish Vaswani et al.

116
00:04:57,630 --> 00:05:00,825
And so that was when
transformers became mainstream.

117
00:05:00,825 --> 00:05:02,700
And then people realized,
OK, like this could

118
00:05:02,700 --> 00:05:04,030
be its own really big thing.

119
00:05:04,030 --> 00:05:06,890
It's a new architecture
that you can use everywhere.

120
00:05:06,890 --> 00:05:09,880
And after that, we saw an
explosion of transformers

121
00:05:09,880 --> 00:05:12,880
into NLP, with BERT, GPT-3.

122
00:05:12,880 --> 00:05:14,720
And then into also other fields.

123
00:05:14,720 --> 00:05:16,510
So now you're seeing
that in like vision,

124
00:05:16,510 --> 00:05:18,223
like protein folding
with Alphafold.

125
00:05:18,223 --> 00:05:19,890
You have all the video
models like Sora.

126
00:05:19,890 --> 00:05:22,695

127
00:05:22,695 --> 00:05:24,070
Everything right
now is basically

128
00:05:24,070 --> 00:05:27,820
some combination of attention,
and some other architectures,

129
00:05:27,820 --> 00:05:30,010
like diffusion, for example.

130
00:05:30,010 --> 00:05:34,600
And this has now led to the
start of this generative AI

131
00:05:34,600 --> 00:05:37,660
era, where now you have all
this powerful models, which

132
00:05:37,660 --> 00:05:39,890
are billion parameters,
trillion parameters.

133
00:05:39,890 --> 00:05:42,770
And then you can use this for a
lot of different applications.

134
00:05:42,770 --> 00:05:44,603
And so if you think
about even like one year

135
00:05:44,603 --> 00:05:46,960
before, AI was very
limited to the lab,

136
00:05:46,960 --> 00:05:49,150
and now you can see AI
has escaped from the lab,

137
00:05:49,150 --> 00:05:50,560
it's now finding real life--

138
00:05:50,560 --> 00:05:51,850
real world applications.

139
00:05:51,850 --> 00:05:57,053
And it has started to
become predominant.

140
00:05:57,053 --> 00:05:58,720
If you look at the
trajectory right now,

141
00:05:58,720 --> 00:06:00,640
it's sort of like we're
on this upward trend,

142
00:06:00,640 --> 00:06:01,900
where we started like
this, and now it's

143
00:06:01,900 --> 00:06:03,650
just growing faster,
and faster, and faster.

144
00:06:03,650 --> 00:06:05,900
And every month, there's just
so many new models coming out.

145
00:06:05,900 --> 00:06:07,390
It's like every day, there's
just so many new things

146
00:06:07,390 --> 00:06:08,060
happening.

147
00:06:08,060 --> 00:06:10,750
And so it's going to be very
exciting to see even a year

148
00:06:10,750 --> 00:06:13,300
or two years from now
how everything changes.

149
00:06:13,300 --> 00:06:16,690
And the society will be led
by this revolutions that are

150
00:06:16,690 --> 00:06:17,980
happening in the field of AI.

151
00:06:17,980 --> 00:06:20,110
So a lot of things are
going to change, and maybe

152
00:06:20,110 --> 00:06:21,970
how we interact
with technology, how

153
00:06:21,970 --> 00:06:24,262
we do things in daily life,
how we have assistants.

154
00:06:24,262 --> 00:06:25,720
And I think a lot
of that will just

155
00:06:25,720 --> 00:06:28,215
come from the things we might
be studying in this class.

156
00:06:28,215 --> 00:06:32,640

157
00:06:32,640 --> 00:06:33,140
Awesome.

158
00:06:33,140 --> 00:06:35,240
Thanks, Div, for going
through the timeline.

159
00:06:35,240 --> 00:06:38,000
So generally, the field of
natural language processing,

160
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:39,650
which is kind of
what transformers

161
00:06:39,650 --> 00:06:42,710
were originally invented for.

162
00:06:42,710 --> 00:06:44,840
The fundamental
discrete nature of text

163
00:06:44,840 --> 00:06:47,300
makes many things difficult.
For example, data augmentation

164
00:06:47,300 --> 00:06:48,170
is more difficult.

165
00:06:48,170 --> 00:06:49,940
You can't just,
for example, flip,

166
00:06:49,940 --> 00:06:53,100
flip it like you flip an image,
change the pixel values a bit.

167
00:06:53,100 --> 00:06:54,470
It's not that simple.

168
00:06:54,470 --> 00:06:56,000
Text is very precise.

169
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,850
One wrong word, changes the
entire meaning of a sentence,

170
00:06:58,850 --> 00:07:01,370
or makes it completely
nonsensical.

171
00:07:01,370 --> 00:07:03,920
And there's also potential
for long context length

172
00:07:03,920 --> 00:07:05,270
as well as memories.

173
00:07:05,270 --> 00:07:07,010
Like, if you're
chatting with ChatGPT

174
00:07:07,010 --> 00:07:09,020
over many different
conversations,

175
00:07:09,020 --> 00:07:11,570
being able to learn and
store all of that information

176
00:07:11,570 --> 00:07:13,280
is a big challenge.

177
00:07:13,280 --> 00:07:15,290
And some of the weaknesses
of earlier models,

178
00:07:15,290 --> 00:07:18,110
which we'll get to later,
short context length,

179
00:07:18,110 --> 00:07:20,612
linear reasoning,
as well as the fact

180
00:07:20,612 --> 00:07:22,070
that many of the
earlier approaches

181
00:07:22,070 --> 00:07:24,710
did not adapt based on context.

182
00:07:24,710 --> 00:07:27,560
So actually, I'll be
running through briefly

183
00:07:27,560 --> 00:07:32,550
how NLP has progressed
throughout the years.

184
00:07:32,550 --> 00:07:35,010
Actually, Seonghee
will be doing that.

185
00:07:35,010 --> 00:07:36,840
Yeah.

186
00:07:36,840 --> 00:07:39,760
While preparing this, I found
this really interesting thing.

187
00:07:39,760 --> 00:07:41,760
This is 1966.

188
00:07:41,760 --> 00:07:44,920
This was the earliest
chatbot called Eliza.

189
00:07:44,920 --> 00:07:47,160
And it wasn't like
a real AI, but it

190
00:07:47,160 --> 00:07:50,760
was more of simulating
patterns of text and words

191
00:07:50,760 --> 00:07:54,330
and sort of creating an
illusion that this chatbot was

192
00:07:54,330 --> 00:07:56,170
sort of understanding
what you were saying.

193
00:07:56,170 --> 00:07:58,680
So these were like the
earliest forms of NLP,

194
00:07:58,680 --> 00:08:00,635
and they were mostly
rule-based approaches

195
00:08:00,635 --> 00:08:02,010
where you're trying
to understand

196
00:08:02,010 --> 00:08:05,280
the patterns in
sentences, patterns

197
00:08:05,280 --> 00:08:07,350
and what words are said.

198
00:08:07,350 --> 00:08:11,460
And these were the earliest
linguistic foundations learning

199
00:08:11,460 --> 00:08:13,260
about semantic parsing.

200
00:08:13,260 --> 00:08:14,550
Yeah.

201
00:08:14,550 --> 00:08:17,340
Then we needed to go
on to understand more

202
00:08:17,340 --> 00:08:19,530
deeper meanings within words.

203
00:08:19,530 --> 00:08:22,020
So we come up with things
called Word Embeddings, which

204
00:08:22,020 --> 00:08:24,810
are vector
representations of words,

205
00:08:24,810 --> 00:08:28,350
and they gave us different
semantic meanings

206
00:08:28,350 --> 00:08:30,670
in words that we weren't
able to understand before.

207
00:08:30,670 --> 00:08:32,659
So we create vector
representations,

208
00:08:32,659 --> 00:08:35,200
words that are similar
appear closer together

209
00:08:35,200 --> 00:08:38,169
in this vector space,
and we're able to learn

210
00:08:38,169 --> 00:08:39,380
different types of meanings.

211
00:08:39,380 --> 00:08:43,390
And then these examples I have
here are word two, vec, love,

212
00:08:43,390 --> 00:08:44,920
bird, Elmo.

213
00:08:44,920 --> 00:08:46,810
These are different
types of word embeddings

214
00:08:46,810 --> 00:08:48,200
and they sort of evolve.

215
00:08:48,200 --> 00:08:50,830
So word two vector is
like a local context word

216
00:08:50,830 --> 00:08:53,230
embedding, word glove, we
get like global context

217
00:08:53,230 --> 00:08:54,910
within documents.

218
00:08:54,910 --> 00:08:57,550
So now that we have
ways to represent words

219
00:08:57,550 --> 00:09:01,420
into vector representations,
now we can turn them into--

220
00:09:01,420 --> 00:09:04,180
put them into our models
and do different types

221
00:09:04,180 --> 00:09:06,970
of tasks, such as
question/answering, or text

222
00:09:06,970 --> 00:09:10,700
summarization, or sentence
completion, machine translation.

223
00:09:10,700 --> 00:09:12,820
And we develop different
types of models

224
00:09:12,820 --> 00:09:14,240
that are able to do that.

225
00:09:14,240 --> 00:09:17,920
So here we have
RNNs, LSTMs that are

226
00:09:17,920 --> 00:09:19,910
used for different
translation tasks.

227
00:09:19,910 --> 00:09:23,470
And so since we have our
models, the now new challenge

228
00:09:23,470 --> 00:09:28,500
becomes to understand how we
can do these tasks better.

229
00:09:28,500 --> 00:09:29,640
So thanks, Seonghee.

230
00:09:29,640 --> 00:09:31,950
So she talked about
sequence to sequence models.

231
00:09:31,950 --> 00:09:35,420
Those are naturally just
inefficient as well as

232
00:09:35,420 --> 00:09:36,890
ineffective for many ways.

233
00:09:36,890 --> 00:09:40,280
You cannot parallelize because
it depends on recurrence.

234
00:09:40,280 --> 00:09:43,130
It relies on maintaining
a hidden context

235
00:09:43,130 --> 00:09:46,730
vector of all the previous
words and their information.

236
00:09:46,730 --> 00:09:49,400
So you couldn't parallelize
and it was inefficient and not

237
00:09:49,400 --> 00:09:50,190
very effective.

238
00:09:50,190 --> 00:09:53,060
So this led to what is now
known as attention as well

239
00:09:53,060 --> 00:09:54,590
as transformers.

240
00:09:54,590 --> 00:09:57,080
So as the word kind
of infers, attention

241
00:09:57,080 --> 00:09:59,720
means being able
to focus attention

242
00:09:59,720 --> 00:10:01,190
to different parts of something.

243
00:10:01,190 --> 00:10:03,270
In this case, a piece of text.

244
00:10:03,270 --> 00:10:06,260
So this is done by using a set
of parameters called weights,

245
00:10:06,260 --> 00:10:08,930
that basically determine
how much attention to should

246
00:10:08,930 --> 00:10:11,750
be paid to each input
at each time step.

247
00:10:11,750 --> 00:10:14,630
And their computer using
a combination of the input

248
00:10:14,630 --> 00:10:16,710
as well as the current
hidden state of the model.

249
00:10:16,710 --> 00:10:19,400
So this will become clearer
as I go through the slides.

250
00:10:19,400 --> 00:10:22,910
But you're here, you
have an example--

251
00:10:22,910 --> 00:10:25,370
or this is an example
of self-attention.

252
00:10:25,370 --> 00:10:27,290
If we're currently
at the word "it,"

253
00:10:27,290 --> 00:10:29,600
we want to know how much
attention do we want to place

254
00:10:29,600 --> 00:10:32,990
to all of the other words
within our input sequence.

255
00:10:32,990 --> 00:10:36,080
And again, this will become
clearer as I explain more.

256
00:10:36,080 --> 00:10:38,540
So the attention
mechanism relies mainly

257
00:10:38,540 --> 00:10:42,170
on these three things called
queries, keys, and values.

258
00:10:42,170 --> 00:10:44,630
So I tried to come up with
a good analogy for this.

259
00:10:44,630 --> 00:10:46,890
And it's basically
like a library system.

260
00:10:46,890 --> 00:10:49,670
So let's say your query is
something you're looking for.

261
00:10:49,670 --> 00:10:51,680
For example, a
specific topic, like I

262
00:10:51,680 --> 00:10:55,040
want books about
how to cook a pizza.

263
00:10:55,040 --> 00:10:56,750
And each book in the
library, let's say,

264
00:10:56,750 --> 00:10:59,150
has a key that
helps identify it.

265
00:10:59,150 --> 00:11:01,860
For example, this
book is about cooking.

266
00:11:01,860 --> 00:11:03,960
This book is about transformers.

267
00:11:03,960 --> 00:11:07,700
This book is about movie
stars, and so forth.

268
00:11:07,700 --> 00:11:09,960
And what you do is you can look.

269
00:11:09,960 --> 00:11:12,620
You can match between your query
as well as each of these keys

270
00:11:12,620 --> 00:11:16,670
or summaries to figure
out which books give you

271
00:11:16,670 --> 00:11:18,063
the most information you need.

272
00:11:18,063 --> 00:11:19,730
And that information
is the value, which

273
00:11:19,730 --> 00:11:21,460
you're trying to retrieve.

274
00:11:21,460 --> 00:11:24,420
But here in attention,
we do a soft match.

275
00:11:24,420 --> 00:11:26,460
We're not trying to
retrieve one book.

276
00:11:26,460 --> 00:11:31,320
We want to see what is the
distribution of relevance

277
00:11:31,320 --> 00:11:33,090
or importance across all books.

278
00:11:33,090 --> 00:11:35,230
For example, this book
might be the most relevant.

279
00:11:35,230 --> 00:11:36,760
I should spend
most of my time on.

280
00:11:36,760 --> 00:11:38,950
This one might be the
second most relevant,

281
00:11:38,950 --> 00:11:41,350
I'll spend a mediocre
amount of time on.

282
00:11:41,350 --> 00:11:44,000
And then book three is less
relevant, and so forth.

283
00:11:44,000 --> 00:11:46,470
So attention is
basically a soft match

284
00:11:46,470 --> 00:11:49,140
between finding
what's most relevant,

285
00:11:49,140 --> 00:11:50,940
which is contained
in those values.

286
00:11:50,940 --> 00:11:53,580
And hence the equation,
when you multiply queries

287
00:11:53,580 --> 00:11:57,720
by keys and then you
multiply that by the values

288
00:11:57,720 --> 00:12:01,320
to get your final attention.

289
00:12:01,320 --> 00:12:03,060
And here's also
just a visualization

290
00:12:03,060 --> 00:12:07,380
from the Illustrated transformer
about how self-attention works.

291
00:12:07,380 --> 00:12:09,810
So you're basically
able to embed your input

292
00:12:09,810 --> 00:12:12,100
words into vectors.

293
00:12:12,100 --> 00:12:15,060
And then for each of these,
you initialize a query key

294
00:12:15,060 --> 00:12:16,450
as well as value matrix.

295
00:12:16,450 --> 00:12:19,920
And these are learned as
the transformers strength.

296
00:12:19,920 --> 00:12:24,180
And you're able to multiply your
inputs by these queries, keys,

297
00:12:24,180 --> 00:12:25,930
and values to get
these final query, key,

298
00:12:25,930 --> 00:12:29,437
and value matrices, which
is then used, again,

299
00:12:29,437 --> 00:12:31,770
as shown in the formula to
calculate the final attention

300
00:12:31,770 --> 00:12:33,000
score.

301
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,430
And the way the
transformer works is it

302
00:12:35,430 --> 00:12:38,790
basically uses attention,
but in a way that's

303
00:12:38,790 --> 00:12:40,950
called multi-head attention,
as in we do attention

304
00:12:40,950 --> 00:12:42,060
several times.

305
00:12:42,060 --> 00:12:46,650
Because since each one
is randomly initialized,

306
00:12:46,650 --> 00:12:49,140
our goal is that each
head of attention

307
00:12:49,140 --> 00:12:52,580
will learned something
useful but different

308
00:12:52,580 --> 00:12:53,610
from the other heads.

309
00:12:53,610 --> 00:12:56,720
So this allows you
to get a more sort

310
00:12:56,720 --> 00:13:01,160
of overarching representation of
potentially relevant information

311
00:13:01,160 --> 00:13:03,080
from your text.

312
00:13:03,080 --> 00:13:05,670
And you see these blocks
are repeated N times.

313
00:13:05,670 --> 00:13:08,300
The point there is once
the multi-head attention is

314
00:13:08,300 --> 00:13:09,770
calculated, so the
attention scores

315
00:13:09,770 --> 00:13:12,440
are calculated from each head,
they're then concatenated.

316
00:13:12,440 --> 00:13:15,260
And then this process is
repeated several times

317
00:13:15,260 --> 00:13:19,340
to potentially learn things like
hierarchical features and more

318
00:13:19,340 --> 00:13:21,350
in-depth information.

319
00:13:21,350 --> 00:13:24,020
And here you'll see this
transformer diagram has

320
00:13:24,020 --> 00:13:26,120
both an encoder and decoder.

321
00:13:26,120 --> 00:13:29,270
This is for something,
for example, T5 or BART,

322
00:13:29,270 --> 00:13:32,600
which is an encoder/decoder
model used for things

323
00:13:32,600 --> 00:13:34,220
like machine translation.

324
00:13:34,220 --> 00:13:37,070
On the other hand, things
like GPT or ChatGPT,

325
00:13:37,070 --> 00:13:41,690
that's simply a
decoder-only, because there's

326
00:13:41,690 --> 00:13:44,307
no second source of
input text, compared

327
00:13:44,307 --> 00:13:45,890
to something like
machine translation,

328
00:13:45,890 --> 00:13:48,260
where you have a sentence
in English which you

329
00:13:48,260 --> 00:13:49,880
want to translate to French.

330
00:13:49,880 --> 00:13:52,140
When you're decoding for
an autoregressive left

331
00:13:52,140 --> 00:13:54,570
to right language
model like ChatGPT,

332
00:13:54,570 --> 00:13:57,690
it basically only has what
has been generated so far.

333
00:13:57,690 --> 00:14:01,380
So that's the difference between
decoder-only and encoder/decoder

334
00:14:01,380 --> 00:14:02,380
transformers.

335
00:14:02,380 --> 00:14:04,230
And the way multi-head
attention works

336
00:14:04,230 --> 00:14:07,620
is you initialize a different
set of queries, keys,

337
00:14:07,620 --> 00:14:11,010
and values, these different
matrices per head, which are all

338
00:14:11,010 --> 00:14:14,340
learned separately as you
train and back propagate across

339
00:14:14,340 --> 00:14:15,190
tons of data.

340
00:14:15,190 --> 00:14:17,550
So again, you embed
each word, split these

341
00:14:17,550 --> 00:14:21,660
into heads, so
separate matrices,

342
00:14:21,660 --> 00:14:23,580
and then you kind of
multiply those together

343
00:14:23,580 --> 00:14:25,740
to get the final
resulting attention, which

344
00:14:25,740 --> 00:14:28,020
are then concatenated
and multiplied

345
00:14:28,020 --> 00:14:29,820
by a final weight matrix.

346
00:14:29,820 --> 00:14:32,280
And then there's some linear
layer and then some softmax

347
00:14:32,280 --> 00:14:34,860
to help you predict the
next token, for example.

348
00:14:34,860 --> 00:14:39,660
So that's the general gist of
how multi-head attention works.

349
00:14:39,660 --> 00:14:42,270
If you want a more in-depth
description of this,

350
00:14:42,270 --> 00:14:46,410
there's lots of resources
online as well as other courses.

351
00:14:46,410 --> 00:14:49,060
And I'll briefly touch upon,
like I said, cross-attention.

352
00:14:49,060 --> 00:14:51,690
So here you have actually
an input sequence

353
00:14:51,690 --> 00:14:53,580
and a different output sequence.

354
00:14:53,580 --> 00:14:56,710
For example, translating
from French to English.

355
00:14:56,710 --> 00:14:59,970
So here, when you're
decoding your output,

356
00:14:59,970 --> 00:15:02,340
your English translated
text, there's

357
00:15:02,340 --> 00:15:04,020
two sources of attention.

358
00:15:04,020 --> 00:15:06,730
One is from the encoder.

359
00:15:06,730 --> 00:15:11,350
So the entire encoded
hidden state of the input.

360
00:15:11,350 --> 00:15:12,810
And that's called
cross-attention

361
00:15:12,810 --> 00:15:15,070
because it's between two
separate pieces of text.

362
00:15:15,070 --> 00:15:20,220
Your queries here are your
current decoded outputs.

363
00:15:20,220 --> 00:15:23,470
And your keys and values
actually come from the encoder.

364
00:15:23,470 --> 00:15:25,510
But there's a second
source of attention,

365
00:15:25,510 --> 00:15:29,350
which is self-attention between
the decoded words themselves.

366
00:15:29,350 --> 00:15:32,040
So they're the queries,
keys, and values are entirely

367
00:15:32,040 --> 00:15:33,280
from the decoded side.

368
00:15:33,280 --> 00:15:36,600
And these types of architectures
combine both types of attention.

369
00:15:36,600 --> 00:15:39,270
Compared to, like I said,
a decoder-only model, which

370
00:15:39,270 --> 00:15:45,880
would only have self-attention
among its own tokens.

371
00:15:45,880 --> 00:15:52,090
And so how exactly are
transformers compared with RNNs?

372
00:15:52,090 --> 00:15:54,220
So RNNs or recurrent
neural networks,

373
00:15:54,220 --> 00:15:57,610
they had issues representing
long range dependencies.

374
00:15:57,610 --> 00:16:00,440
There were issues with gradient
vanishing, as well as explosion.

375
00:16:00,440 --> 00:16:03,160
Since you're concatenating
all of this information

376
00:16:03,160 --> 00:16:06,590
into one single hidden vector,
this leads to a lot of issues

377
00:16:06,590 --> 00:16:07,582
potentially.

378
00:16:07,582 --> 00:16:09,790
There were a large number
of training steps involved,

379
00:16:09,790 --> 00:16:11,440
and like I said, you
can't parallelize

380
00:16:11,440 --> 00:16:14,140
because it's sequential
and relies on recurrence.

381
00:16:14,140 --> 00:16:17,390
Whereas transformers can
model long range dependencies.

382
00:16:17,390 --> 00:16:20,470
There's no gradient vanishing
or exploding problem.

383
00:16:20,470 --> 00:16:22,570
And it can be parallelized.

384
00:16:22,570 --> 00:16:24,820
For example, to take
more advantage of things

385
00:16:24,820 --> 00:16:26,560
like GPU compute.

386
00:16:26,560 --> 00:16:28,690
So overall, it's much
more efficient and also

387
00:16:28,690 --> 00:16:31,510
much more effective at
representing language.

388
00:16:31,510 --> 00:16:34,900
And hence why it's one of the
most popular deep learning

389
00:16:34,900 --> 00:16:37,940
architectures today.

390
00:16:37,940 --> 00:16:40,340
So large language
models are basically a

391
00:16:40,340 --> 00:16:43,100
scaled up version of this
transformer architecture.

392
00:16:43,100 --> 00:16:45,230
Up to millions or
billions of parameters.

393
00:16:45,230 --> 00:16:46,970
And parameters
here are basically

394
00:16:46,970 --> 00:16:48,860
nodes in the neural network.

395
00:16:48,860 --> 00:16:51,850
They're typically trained on
massive amounts of general text

396
00:16:51,850 --> 00:16:52,350
data.

397
00:16:52,350 --> 00:16:55,070
For example, mining a bunch of
text from Wikipedia, Reddit,

398
00:16:55,070 --> 00:16:56,180
and so forth.

399
00:16:56,180 --> 00:17:00,090
Typically there are processes
to filter this text.

400
00:17:00,090 --> 00:17:02,390
For example, getting rid
of Nazi for work things

401
00:17:02,390 --> 00:17:04,230
and general quality filters.

402
00:17:04,230 --> 00:17:07,440
And the training objective is
typically next token prediction.

403
00:17:07,440 --> 00:17:11,000
So again, it's to predict the
next token, or the most probable

404
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,710
next token given all
of the previous tokens.

405
00:17:13,710 --> 00:17:15,770
So again, this is how
the autoregressive

406
00:17:15,770 --> 00:17:19,333
left to right architecture
like ChatGPT works.

407
00:17:19,333 --> 00:17:20,750
And it's also been
shown that they

408
00:17:20,750 --> 00:17:22,730
have emergent abilities
as they scale up,

409
00:17:22,730 --> 00:17:24,589
which Emily will talk about.

410
00:17:24,589 --> 00:17:27,150
However, they have heavy
computational cost.

411
00:17:27,150 --> 00:17:29,300
Training these huge
networks on tons of data

412
00:17:29,300 --> 00:17:31,530
takes a lot of time,
money, and GPUs.

413
00:17:31,530 --> 00:17:33,980
And it's also led to the
fact that this can only

414
00:17:33,980 --> 00:17:35,900
be done effectively at
big companies, which

415
00:17:35,900 --> 00:17:38,610
have these resources
as well as money.

416
00:17:38,610 --> 00:17:42,270
And what's happened now is we
have very general models, which

417
00:17:42,270 --> 00:17:44,970
you can use and plug
and play and use them

418
00:17:44,970 --> 00:17:48,600
on very on different
tasks without needing

419
00:17:48,600 --> 00:17:51,630
to retrain them using things
like in-context, learning,

420
00:17:51,630 --> 00:17:54,480
transfer learning,
as well as prompting.

421
00:17:54,480 --> 00:17:57,860
And now Emily will
talk about emergent.

422
00:17:57,860 --> 00:17:58,360
Yeah.

423
00:17:58,360 --> 00:18:02,200
So I guess a natural
question for why

424
00:18:02,200 --> 00:18:05,600
language models work so well, is
what happens when you scale up.

425
00:18:05,600 --> 00:18:07,720
And as we've seen
in the past, there's

426
00:18:07,720 --> 00:18:11,290
been this big trend of investing
more money into our compute,

427
00:18:11,290 --> 00:18:13,523
making our models larger,
and larger, and larger.

428
00:18:13,523 --> 00:18:15,940
And actually we have seen some
really cool things come out

429
00:18:15,940 --> 00:18:20,050
of it, which we have now
termed emergent abilities.

430
00:18:20,050 --> 00:18:23,680
We can call emergent
abilities, an ability

431
00:18:23,680 --> 00:18:28,180
that is present in a larger
model, but not in a smaller one.

432
00:18:28,180 --> 00:18:31,030
And I think the thing that is
most interesting about this

433
00:18:31,030 --> 00:18:34,100
is emergent abilities
are very unpredictable.

434
00:18:34,100 --> 00:18:36,430
It's not necessarily like
we have a scaling law

435
00:18:36,430 --> 00:18:38,890
that we just keep training,
and training, and training

436
00:18:38,890 --> 00:18:43,510
this model and we can say,
oh, at this training step,

437
00:18:43,510 --> 00:18:45,950
we'll have this ability to
do this really cool thing.

438
00:18:45,950 --> 00:18:48,890
It's actually something more
like, it's kind of random.

439
00:18:48,890 --> 00:18:50,800
And then at this
threshold that is

440
00:18:50,800 --> 00:18:54,530
pretty difficult or impossible
to predict, it just improves.

441
00:18:54,530 --> 00:18:57,030
And we call that a
phase transition.

442
00:18:57,030 --> 00:19:00,420
And this is a figure
taken by a paper authored

443
00:19:00,420 --> 00:19:03,270
by a speaker we'll
have next week--

444
00:19:03,270 --> 00:19:05,790
Jason Wei, who I'm very
excited to hear from,

445
00:19:05,790 --> 00:19:08,100
and he did this really
cool research project

446
00:19:08,100 --> 00:19:10,770
with a bunch of
other people, sort

447
00:19:10,770 --> 00:19:14,700
of characterizing and exhibiting
a lot of the emergent abilities

448
00:19:14,700 --> 00:19:17,020
that you can notice
in different models.

449
00:19:17,020 --> 00:19:19,860
So here we have five
different models.

450
00:19:19,860 --> 00:19:22,830
And a lot of
different common tasks

451
00:19:22,830 --> 00:19:27,550
that we test language models on
to see what their abilities are.

452
00:19:27,550 --> 00:19:32,460
And so, for example, complicated
arithmetic, or transliteration,

453
00:19:32,460 --> 00:19:34,920
being able to tell
if someone is telling

454
00:19:34,920 --> 00:19:37,000
the truth, other
things like this.

455
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
And as you can notice
on this figure,

456
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:44,690
we have these eight graphs, and
there's this very obvious spike.

457
00:19:44,690 --> 00:19:50,050
It's not necessarily like this
gradual increase in accuracy.

458
00:19:50,050 --> 00:19:55,220
And so that is sort of what we
can term that phase transition.

459
00:19:55,220 --> 00:19:57,860
And currently there's
very few explanations

460
00:19:57,860 --> 00:20:00,140
for why these abilities emerge.

461
00:20:00,140 --> 00:20:02,750
Evaluation metrics used to
measure these abilities,

462
00:20:02,750 --> 00:20:05,900
don't fully explain
why they emerge.

463
00:20:05,900 --> 00:20:08,240
And an interesting
research paper

464
00:20:08,240 --> 00:20:10,970
that came out recently by
some researchers at Stanford

465
00:20:10,970 --> 00:20:14,660
actually claimed that maybe
emergent abilities of LLMs

466
00:20:14,660 --> 00:20:16,350
are nonexistent.

467
00:20:16,350 --> 00:20:19,700
Maybe it's more so
the researchers choice

468
00:20:19,700 --> 00:20:22,400
of metric being
nonlinear rather than

469
00:20:22,400 --> 00:20:28,110
fundamental changes in the
model responding to the scale.

470
00:20:28,110 --> 00:20:34,600
And so a natural question is,
is scaling the best thing to do?

471
00:20:34,600 --> 00:20:36,120
Is it the only thing to do?

472
00:20:36,120 --> 00:20:40,380
Is it the most significant way
that we can improve our models?

473
00:20:40,380 --> 00:20:43,560
And so while scaling is a factor
in these emergent abilities,

474
00:20:43,560 --> 00:20:46,780
it is not the only factor,
especially in smaller models.

475
00:20:46,780 --> 00:20:50,100
We have new architectures,
higher quality data,

476
00:20:50,100 --> 00:20:54,030
and improved training procedures
that could potentially

477
00:20:54,030 --> 00:20:56,930
bring about these emergent
abilities on smaller models.

478
00:20:56,930 --> 00:21:00,300
And so these present a lot of
interesting research directions,

479
00:21:00,300 --> 00:21:02,820
including improving
few-shot prompting abilities

480
00:21:02,820 --> 00:21:05,820
as we've seen before
through other methods,

481
00:21:05,820 --> 00:21:08,460
and theoretical and
interpretability

482
00:21:08,460 --> 00:21:12,390
research, computational
linguistics work, and yeah,

483
00:21:12,390 --> 00:21:14,100
other directions.

484
00:21:14,100 --> 00:21:16,590
And so as some
interesting questions,

485
00:21:16,590 --> 00:21:19,350
do you believe that emergent
abilities will continue

486
00:21:19,350 --> 00:21:21,660
to arise with more scale?

487
00:21:21,660 --> 00:21:25,290
Is there like maybe once
we get to some crazy number

488
00:21:25,290 --> 00:21:27,618
of parameters, then
our language models

489
00:21:27,618 --> 00:21:29,410
will suddenly be able
to think on their own

490
00:21:29,410 --> 00:21:32,830
and do all sorts of cool things,
or is there some sort of limit.

491
00:21:32,830 --> 00:21:34,690
What are your thoughts
on this current trend

492
00:21:34,690 --> 00:21:36,540
of larger models and more data?

493
00:21:36,540 --> 00:21:39,430

494
00:21:39,430 --> 00:21:41,180
Is this a good direction?

495
00:21:41,180 --> 00:21:44,020
Larger models obviously mean
more money, more compute,

496
00:21:44,020 --> 00:21:48,670
and Less democratization
of AI research.

497
00:21:48,670 --> 00:21:51,100
And thoughts on retrieval
based or retrieval

498
00:21:51,100 --> 00:21:54,040
augmented systems compared
to simply learning everything

499
00:21:54,040 --> 00:21:55,540
within the parameters
of the model.

500
00:21:55,540 --> 00:21:57,170
So lots of cool directions.

501
00:21:57,170 --> 00:22:01,390

502
00:22:01,390 --> 00:22:01,890
Yeah.

503
00:22:01,890 --> 00:22:05,040
So we have some quick
introductions on reinforcement

504
00:22:05,040 --> 00:22:06,390
learning from human feedback.

505
