753
00:33:11,520 --> 00:33:13,230
We learn while
growing up by talking

754
00:33:13,230 --> 00:33:16,050
to our parents,
teachers, other children.

755
00:33:16,050 --> 00:33:17,850
This is not just
basic things, but even

756
00:33:17,850 --> 00:33:20,940
things like values, human values
to treat others with kindness

757
00:33:20,940 --> 00:33:22,170
and so forth.

758
00:33:22,170 --> 00:33:24,420
And this is not
something that LLM

759
00:33:24,420 --> 00:33:27,070
is really exposed to
when it's trained on just

760
00:33:27,070 --> 00:33:30,610
large amounts of text data.

761
00:33:30,610 --> 00:33:34,990
Kind of related is this trend
towards smaller open source

762
00:33:34,990 --> 00:33:36,880
models, potentially
things we can even

763
00:33:36,880 --> 00:33:39,910
run on our everyday devices.

764
00:33:39,910 --> 00:33:42,280
For example, there's more
and more work on AutoGPT

765
00:33:42,280 --> 00:33:44,560
as well as ChatGPT plugins.

766
00:33:44,560 --> 00:33:46,840
Smaller open source models
like LLaMA, as well as

767
00:33:46,840 --> 00:33:48,080
Mistral models.

768
00:33:48,080 --> 00:33:49,630
And in the future,
hopefully we'll

769
00:33:49,630 --> 00:33:54,790
be able to finetune and run even
more models locally, potentially

770
00:33:54,790 --> 00:33:56,570
even on our smartphone.

771
00:33:56,570 --> 00:33:59,890

772
00:33:59,890 --> 00:34:02,410
Another area of
research and work

773
00:34:02,410 --> 00:34:05,780
is in memory augmentation
as well as personalization.

774
00:34:05,780 --> 00:34:08,230
So current big
weakness of LLMs is

775
00:34:08,230 --> 00:34:09,790
they're sort of
frozen in knowledge

776
00:34:09,790 --> 00:34:11,429
at a particular point in time.

777
00:34:11,429 --> 00:34:13,600
They don't augment
knowledge on the fly

778
00:34:13,600 --> 00:34:16,239
as they're talking to you.

779
00:34:16,239 --> 00:34:18,887
It's not stored into their
brain, the parameters.

780
00:34:18,887 --> 00:34:20,679
The next time you start
a new conversation,

781
00:34:20,679 --> 00:34:22,570
there's a very high
chance it won't remember

782
00:34:22,570 --> 00:34:25,750
anything you said before.

783
00:34:25,750 --> 00:34:27,429
I'll get to RAG in a bit.

784
00:34:27,429 --> 00:34:30,310
So one of our goals
hopefully in the future

785
00:34:30,310 --> 00:34:33,969
is to have this wide scale
memory augmentation as well

786
00:34:33,969 --> 00:34:35,110
as personalization.

787
00:34:35,110 --> 00:34:38,409
Somehow update the
model on the fly

788
00:34:38,409 --> 00:34:40,989
while talking to hundreds or
thousands or millions of users

789
00:34:40,989 --> 00:34:42,460
around the world.

790
00:34:42,460 --> 00:34:44,860
And to adapt not
only their knowledge,

791
00:34:44,860 --> 00:34:47,170
but their talking style
as well as persona

792
00:34:47,170 --> 00:34:48,679
to the particular user.

793
00:34:48,679 --> 00:34:50,469
And this is called
personalization.

794
00:34:50,469 --> 00:34:52,460
This could have many
different applications,

795
00:34:52,460 --> 00:34:56,360
such as mental health
therapy, and so forth.

796
00:34:56,360 --> 00:34:58,220
So some potential
approaches for this

797
00:34:58,220 --> 00:35:00,320
could be having a memory bank.

798
00:35:00,320 --> 00:35:03,050
This is not that feasible
with larger amounts of data.

799
00:35:03,050 --> 00:35:06,980
Prefix-tuning approaches, which
finetunes only a very small

800
00:35:06,980 --> 00:35:08,140
portion of the model.

801
00:35:08,140 --> 00:35:10,190
However, when you
have such huge LLMs,

802
00:35:10,190 --> 00:35:12,350
even finetuning a very
small portion of the model

803
00:35:12,350 --> 00:35:14,420
is incredibly expensive.

804
00:35:14,420 --> 00:35:17,840
Maybe some prompt-based
approaches in context learning.

805
00:35:17,840 --> 00:35:21,870
However, again, this will
not change the model itself.

806
00:35:21,870 --> 00:35:26,210
It will likely not carry forward
among different conversations.

807
00:35:26,210 --> 00:35:27,920
And there's this
thing now called

808
00:35:27,920 --> 00:35:30,232
RAG-- retrieval-augmented
generation, which

809
00:35:30,232 --> 00:35:31,940
is related to memory
bank, where you have

810
00:35:31,940 --> 00:35:33,810
a data store of information.

811
00:35:33,810 --> 00:35:37,010
And each time when the user
puts in an input query,

812
00:35:37,010 --> 00:35:39,350
you first look at if
there's relevant information

813
00:35:39,350 --> 00:35:41,120
from this data store,
that you can then

814
00:35:41,120 --> 00:35:46,370
augment as context into LLM
to help guide its output.

815
00:35:46,370 --> 00:35:50,510
This relies on having a high
quality external data store.

816
00:35:50,510 --> 00:35:53,330
And it's also typically
not end-to-end.

817
00:35:53,330 --> 00:35:55,310
And the main thing
here is it's not

818
00:35:55,310 --> 00:35:58,530
within the brain of
the model, but outside.

819
00:35:58,530 --> 00:36:01,470
It's suitable for knowledge
or fact-based information.

820
00:36:01,470 --> 00:36:03,660
But it's not really
suitable for enhancing

821
00:36:03,660 --> 00:36:08,600
the fundamental capabilities
or skills of the model.

822
00:36:08,600 --> 00:36:13,130
There's also lots of work now
on pre-training data synthesis,

823
00:36:13,130 --> 00:36:16,020
especially after ChatGPT
and GPT-4 came out.

824
00:36:16,020 --> 00:36:17,510
Instead of having
to collect data

825
00:36:17,510 --> 00:36:20,390
from humans, which can be very
expensive and time-consuming,

826
00:36:20,390 --> 00:36:23,450
many researchers now are
using GPT-4 for example,

827
00:36:23,450 --> 00:36:26,360
to collect data, to
train other models.

828
00:36:26,360 --> 00:36:30,050
For example, model
distillation, training

829
00:36:30,050 --> 00:36:33,260
smaller and less
capable models with data

830
00:36:33,260 --> 00:36:35,720
from larger models like GPT-4.

831
00:36:35,720 --> 00:36:38,300
An example is the
Microsoft Phi models

832
00:36:38,300 --> 00:36:39,690
introduced from their paper--

833
00:36:39,690 --> 00:36:42,090
Textbooks Are All You Need.

834
00:36:42,090 --> 00:36:44,160
And speaking a bit more
about the Phi model,

835
00:36:44,160 --> 00:36:48,150
it's a 2.7 billion parameter
model, Phi version 2,

836
00:36:48,150 --> 00:36:50,970
and it excels in
reasoning in language.

837
00:36:50,970 --> 00:36:53,490
Challenging or having
comparative performance

838
00:36:53,490 --> 00:36:57,180
to compare two models
up to 25 times larger,

839
00:36:57,180 --> 00:37:00,030
which is incredibly impressive.

840
00:37:00,030 --> 00:37:04,860
And their main takeaway here
is the quality or source

841
00:37:04,860 --> 00:37:07,060
of data is incredibly important.

842
00:37:07,060 --> 00:37:09,690
So they emphasize textbook
quality training data

843
00:37:09,690 --> 00:37:11,490
and synthetic data.

844
00:37:11,490 --> 00:37:14,010
They generated synthetic
data to teach the model

845
00:37:14,010 --> 00:37:16,090
common sense reasoning
and general knowledge.

846
00:37:16,090 --> 00:37:18,540
This includes things like
science, daily activities,

847
00:37:18,540 --> 00:37:20,400
theory of mind, and so forth.

848
00:37:20,400 --> 00:37:23,340
They then augmented this with
additional data collected

849
00:37:23,340 --> 00:37:25,560
from the web that
was filtered-based

850
00:37:25,560 --> 00:37:29,100
on educational value as
well as content quality.

851
00:37:29,100 --> 00:37:32,310
And what this allowed them to do
is train a much smaller model,

852
00:37:32,310 --> 00:37:36,510
much more efficiently,
while challenging models up

853
00:37:36,510 --> 00:37:40,710
to 25 times larger, which
is, again, very impressive.

854
00:37:40,710 --> 00:37:43,930
Another area of debate is
are LLMs truly learning.

855
00:37:43,930 --> 00:37:45,280
Are they learning new knowledge?

856
00:37:45,280 --> 00:37:46,863
When you ask it to
do something, is it

857
00:37:46,863 --> 00:37:48,000
generating it from scratch?

858
00:37:48,000 --> 00:37:53,560
Or is it simply regurgitating,
something it's memorized before?

859
00:37:53,560 --> 00:37:56,100
This line has been blurred
and it's not clear,

860
00:37:56,100 --> 00:37:59,100
because the way LLMs learn is
from, again, learning patterns

861
00:37:59,100 --> 00:38:03,930
from lots of texts, which you
can say is somewhat memorizing.

862
00:38:03,930 --> 00:38:06,870
There's also the potential
for test time contamination.

863
00:38:06,870 --> 00:38:10,500
Models might
regurgitate information,

864
00:38:10,500 --> 00:38:12,930
it's seen during training
while being evaluated,

865
00:38:12,930 --> 00:38:16,410
and this can lead to
misleading benchmark results.

866
00:38:16,410 --> 00:38:18,360
There's also
cognitive simulation.

867
00:38:18,360 --> 00:38:22,620
So a lot of people arguing
that LLMs mimic human thought

868
00:38:22,620 --> 00:38:25,390
processes, while others say no.

869
00:38:25,390 --> 00:38:27,750
It's just a sophisticated
form of pattern matching,

870
00:38:27,750 --> 00:38:31,650
and it's not nearly as
complex or biological

871
00:38:31,650 --> 00:38:34,500
or sophisticated as a human.

872
00:38:34,500 --> 00:38:37,740
And this also leads to a lot
of ethical as well as practical

873
00:38:37,740 --> 00:38:38,710
limitations.

874
00:38:38,710 --> 00:38:40,240
So, for example,
I'm sure you've all

875
00:38:40,240 --> 00:38:43,360
heard that recent lawsuit,
copyright lawsuit by New York

876
00:38:43,360 --> 00:38:48,010
Times on OpenAI, where they
claim that OpenAIs ChatGPT was

877
00:38:48,010 --> 00:38:51,200
basically regurgitating existing
New York Times articles.

878
00:38:51,200 --> 00:38:54,520
And this is again,
this issue with LLMs

879
00:38:54,520 --> 00:38:57,700
potentially memorizing
text it saw during training

880
00:38:57,700 --> 00:39:01,540
rather than synthesizing
new information entirely

881
00:39:01,540 --> 00:39:02,205
from scratch.

882
00:39:02,205 --> 00:39:04,970

883
00:39:04,970 --> 00:39:09,242
Another big source
of challenge, which

884
00:39:09,242 --> 00:39:11,450
might be able to close the
gap between current models

885
00:39:11,450 --> 00:39:15,770
and eventually maybe
AGI is this concept

886
00:39:15,770 --> 00:39:18,500
of continual learning,
AKA, infinite

887
00:39:18,500 --> 00:39:21,440
and permanent fundamental
self-improvement.

888
00:39:21,440 --> 00:39:24,110
So humans were able to
learn constantly, every day

889
00:39:24,110 --> 00:39:25,490
from every interaction.

890
00:39:25,490 --> 00:39:27,800
I'm learning right now
from just talking to you

891
00:39:27,800 --> 00:39:29,520
and giving this lecture.

892
00:39:29,520 --> 00:39:31,560
We don't need to
finetune ourselves.

893
00:39:31,560 --> 00:39:34,010
We don't need to sit-in a
chair and then have someone

894
00:39:34,010 --> 00:39:36,890
read the whole internet
to us every two months

895
00:39:36,890 --> 00:39:39,080
or something like that.

896
00:39:39,080 --> 00:39:40,820
Currently, there's
work on finetuning,

897
00:39:40,820 --> 00:39:43,760
a small model based on
traces from a better model,

898
00:39:43,760 --> 00:39:46,910
or the same model after
filtering those traces.

899
00:39:46,910 --> 00:39:49,580
However, this is closer to
retraining and distillation

900
00:39:49,580 --> 00:39:53,930
than it is to true human
continual learning.

901
00:39:53,930 --> 00:39:57,440
So that's definitely, I think,
at least a very exciting

902
00:39:57,440 --> 00:39:59,920
direction.

903
00:39:59,920 --> 00:40:04,210
Another area of challenge is
interpreting these huge LLMs

904
00:40:04,210 --> 00:40:06,450
with billions of parameters.

905
00:40:06,450 --> 00:40:08,530
They're essentially
huge black-box models,

906
00:40:08,530 --> 00:40:10,030
where it's really
hard to understand

907
00:40:10,030 --> 00:40:13,000
exactly what is going on.

908
00:40:13,000 --> 00:40:15,280
If we were able to
understand them better,

909
00:40:15,280 --> 00:40:17,410
this would allow us to
know what exactly we

910
00:40:17,410 --> 00:40:19,270
should try to improve.

911
00:40:19,270 --> 00:40:23,210
It also allows us to
control these models better,

912
00:40:23,210 --> 00:40:26,140
and potentially lead to better
alignment as well as safety.

913
00:40:26,140 --> 00:40:29,290
And there's this
area of work called

914
00:40:29,290 --> 00:40:31,330
mechanistic
interpretability, which

915
00:40:31,330 --> 00:40:34,570
tries to understand exactly how
the individual components, as

916
00:40:34,570 --> 00:40:37,210
well as operations in a
machine learning model

917
00:40:37,210 --> 00:40:40,420
contribute to its overall
decision-making process.

918
00:40:40,420 --> 00:40:45,980
And to try to unpack
that black-box, I guess.

919
00:40:45,980 --> 00:40:49,010
So speaking a bit more
about a concept related

920
00:40:49,010 --> 00:40:52,130
to mechanistic interpretability
as well as continual learning

921
00:40:52,130 --> 00:40:53,700
is model editing.

922
00:40:53,700 --> 00:40:55,490
So this is a newer
line of work which

923
00:40:55,490 --> 00:40:58,010
hasn't seen too much
investigation, also because it's

924
00:40:58,010 --> 00:40:59,708
very challenging.

925
00:40:59,708 --> 00:41:01,250
But basically, this
looks like can we

926
00:41:01,250 --> 00:41:03,320
edit very specific
nodes in the model

927
00:41:03,320 --> 00:41:05,390
without having to retrain it.

928
00:41:05,390 --> 00:41:08,030
So one of the papers
I linked there, they

929
00:41:08,030 --> 00:41:11,570
developed a causal
intervention method to trace

930
00:41:11,570 --> 00:41:14,750
the neural activations for
model factual predictions,

931
00:41:14,750 --> 00:41:16,430
and they came up
with this method

932
00:41:16,430 --> 00:41:18,740
called Rank-One Model
Editing or ROME,

933
00:41:18,740 --> 00:41:21,350
that was able to modify
very specific model

934
00:41:21,350 --> 00:41:24,480
weights for updating
factual associations.

935
00:41:24,480 --> 00:41:28,760
For example, Ottawa is
the capital of Canada

936
00:41:28,760 --> 00:41:30,540
and then modifying
that to something else.

937
00:41:30,540 --> 00:41:33,180
They found they didn't need
to re-finetune the model.

938
00:41:33,180 --> 00:41:36,650
They were able to
inject that information

939
00:41:36,650 --> 00:41:39,440
into the model, pretty
much in a permanent way

940
00:41:39,440 --> 00:41:42,200
by simply modifying
very specific nodes.

941
00:41:42,200 --> 00:41:45,020
They also found that
mid-layer feedforward modules

942
00:41:45,020 --> 00:41:47,550
played a very significant
role in storing

943
00:41:47,550 --> 00:41:50,650
these sorts of factual
information or associations.

944
00:41:50,650 --> 00:41:54,150
And the manipulation of these
can be a feasible approach

945
00:41:54,150 --> 00:41:55,420
for model editing.

946
00:41:55,420 --> 00:41:58,080
So I think this is
a very cool line

947
00:41:58,080 --> 00:42:01,800
of work with potential
long term impacts.

948
00:42:01,800 --> 00:42:04,260
And as Seonghee stated
before, another line of work

949
00:42:04,260 --> 00:42:06,390
is basically mixture of experts.

950
00:42:06,390 --> 00:42:09,210
So this is very prevalent
in current day LLMs,

951
00:42:09,210 --> 00:42:11,220
things like GPT-4 and Gemini.

952
00:42:11,220 --> 00:42:13,920
And so to have several models
or experts work together

953
00:42:13,920 --> 00:42:17,370
to solve a problem, and
arrive at a final generation.

954
00:42:17,370 --> 00:42:18,900
And there's a lot
of research on how

955
00:42:18,900 --> 00:42:21,510
to better define and
initialize these experts

956
00:42:21,510 --> 00:42:27,122
and connect them to come
up with a final result.

957
00:42:27,122 --> 00:42:29,080
And I'm thinking, is
there a way of potentially

958
00:42:29,080 --> 00:42:31,330
having a single model
variation of this similar

959
00:42:31,330 --> 00:42:32,542
to the human brain?

960
00:42:32,542 --> 00:42:34,750
For example, the human brain,
we have different parts

961
00:42:34,750 --> 00:42:36,550
of our brain for
different things.

962
00:42:36,550 --> 00:42:40,750
One part of our brain might work
more for spatial reasoning, one

963
00:42:40,750 --> 00:42:43,790
for physical reasoning, one for
mathematical logical reasoning,

964
00:42:43,790 --> 00:42:44,550
and so forth.

965
00:42:44,550 --> 00:42:46,810
Maybe there's a way of
segmenting a single neural

966
00:42:46,810 --> 00:42:48,400
network or model in such a way.

967
00:42:48,400 --> 00:42:51,460
For example, by adding more
layers on top of a foundation

968
00:42:51,460 --> 00:42:55,300
model, and then only finetuning
those specific layers

969
00:42:55,300 --> 00:42:58,990
for different purposes.

970
00:42:58,990 --> 00:43:02,050
Related to continual learning
is self-improvement as well as

971
00:43:02,050 --> 00:43:03,107
self reflection.

972
00:43:03,107 --> 00:43:04,690
So there's been a
lot of work recently

973
00:43:04,690 --> 00:43:08,080
that's also shown that
models, especially LLMs, they

974
00:43:08,080 --> 00:43:11,170
can reflect on their own output
to iteratively refine as well

975
00:43:11,170 --> 00:43:13,420
as improve them.

976
00:43:13,420 --> 00:43:16,120
It's been shown that
this improvement can

977
00:43:16,120 --> 00:43:20,360
happen across several
layers of self-reflection.

978
00:43:20,360 --> 00:43:24,010
Having a mini version
of continual learning

979
00:43:24,010 --> 00:43:25,450
up to a certain degree.

980
00:43:25,450 --> 00:43:28,360
And some folks believe
that AGI is basically

981
00:43:28,360 --> 00:43:31,330
a constant state
of self-reflection,

982
00:43:31,330 --> 00:43:34,380
which is, again, similar
to what a human does.

983
00:43:34,380 --> 00:43:37,140

984
00:43:37,140 --> 00:43:40,680
Lastly, a big issue is
the hallucination problem,

985
00:43:40,680 --> 00:43:43,500
where a model does not
know what it does not know.

986
00:43:43,500 --> 00:43:45,523
And due to the
sampling procedure,

987
00:43:45,523 --> 00:43:47,190
there's a very high
chance, for example,

988
00:43:47,190 --> 00:43:49,860
I'm sure you've also used
ChatGPT before that it sometimes

989
00:43:49,860 --> 00:43:51,720
generates text, it's
very confident about,

990
00:43:51,720 --> 00:43:54,870
but is simply incorrect,
like factually incorrect

991
00:43:54,870 --> 00:43:56,700
and does not make any sense.

992
00:43:56,700 --> 00:43:59,220
We can potentially enhance
this through different ways.

993
00:43:59,220 --> 00:44:01,800
Maybe some sort of
internal-based fact verification

994
00:44:01,800 --> 00:44:04,355
approach based on
confidence scores.

995
00:44:04,355 --> 00:44:06,480
There's this line of work
called model calibration,

996
00:44:06,480 --> 00:44:10,830
which kind of works on
that, potentially verifying

997
00:44:10,830 --> 00:44:13,230
and regenerating output.

998
00:44:13,230 --> 00:44:16,230
If it finds that its
output is incorrect,

999
00:44:16,230 --> 00:44:20,570
maybe it can be
asked to regenerate.

1000
00:44:20,570 --> 00:44:23,180
And of course, there's things
like RAG-based approaches, where

1001
00:44:23,180 --> 00:44:26,540
you're able to retrieve from a
knowledge store, which is also

1002
00:44:26,540 --> 00:44:30,080
a potential solution people
have investigated for reducing

1003
00:44:30,080 --> 00:44:34,540
this problem of hallucination.

1004
00:44:34,540 --> 00:44:38,660
Lastly, Emily will touch upon
some chain of thought reasoning.

1005
00:44:38,660 --> 00:44:39,170
Yeah.

1006
00:44:39,170 --> 00:44:41,930
So chain of thought
is something I

1007
00:44:41,930 --> 00:44:43,610
think is really
cool because I think

1008
00:44:43,610 --> 00:44:49,310
it combines the cognitive
imitation and also

1009
00:44:49,310 --> 00:44:52,050
interpretability
lines of research.

1010
00:44:52,050 --> 00:44:54,260
And so chain of
thought is the idea

1011
00:44:54,260 --> 00:44:58,070
that all of us, unless you have
some extraordinary photographic

1012
00:44:58,070 --> 00:45:01,100
memory, think through
things step by step.

1013
00:45:01,100 --> 00:45:04,700
If I asked you to multiply a
10 digit number by a 10 digit

1014
00:45:04,700 --> 00:45:06,950
number, you'd probably
have to break that down

1015
00:45:06,950 --> 00:45:09,140
into intermediate
reasoning steps.

1016
00:45:09,140 --> 00:45:11,330
And so some researchers
thought, well,

1017
00:45:11,330 --> 00:45:13,940
what if we do the same things
with large language models

1018
00:45:13,940 --> 00:45:17,300
and see if forcing
them to reason

1019
00:45:17,300 --> 00:45:19,640
through their ideas and
their thoughts helps

1020
00:45:19,640 --> 00:45:22,640
them have better accuracy
and better results.

1021
00:45:22,640 --> 00:45:25,010
And so chain of thought
exploits the idea

1022
00:45:25,010 --> 00:45:29,510
that ultimately these models
have these weights that

1023
00:45:29,510 --> 00:45:32,030
know more about a
problem rather than just

1024
00:45:32,030 --> 00:45:39,360
having it prompt and regurgitate
what just to get a response.

1025
00:45:39,360 --> 00:45:41,880
And so an example of
chain of thought reasoning

1026
00:45:41,880 --> 00:45:43,780
is on the right.

1027
00:45:43,780 --> 00:45:46,480
So as you can see on the left,
there's standard prompting.

1028
00:45:46,480 --> 00:45:49,030
So I give you this
complicated question.

1029
00:45:49,030 --> 00:45:51,490
Let's say we're doing
this entirely new problem.

1030
00:45:51,490 --> 00:45:54,760
I give you the question and
I just give you the answer,

1031
00:45:54,760 --> 00:45:56,350
I don't tell you how to do it.

1032
00:45:56,350 --> 00:45:57,840
That's kind of difficult.

1033
00:45:57,840 --> 00:45:59,410
Versus chain of thought.

1034
00:45:59,410 --> 00:46:01,560
The first example that
you get, I actually

1035
00:46:01,560 --> 00:46:04,020
walk you through the answer.

1036
00:46:04,020 --> 00:46:06,210
And then the idea
is that hopefully,

1037
00:46:06,210 --> 00:46:09,540
since you have this framework of
how to think about a question,

1038
00:46:09,540 --> 00:46:13,920
you're able to produce
a more accurate output.

1039
00:46:13,920 --> 00:46:17,760
And so chain of thought
resulted in pretty significant

1040
00:46:17,760 --> 00:46:21,600
performance gains for
larger language models.

1041
00:46:21,600 --> 00:46:23,340
Similarly to what I
touched upon before,

1042
00:46:23,340 --> 00:46:25,450
this is an emergent ability.

1043
00:46:25,450 --> 00:46:28,650
And so we don't really
see the same performance

1044
00:46:28,650 --> 00:46:30,450
for smaller models.

1045
00:46:30,450 --> 00:46:32,640
But something that I
think is important,

1046
00:46:32,640 --> 00:46:35,340
as I mentioned before, is
this idea of interpretability.

1047
00:46:35,340 --> 00:46:38,220
Because we can see
this model's output

1048
00:46:38,220 --> 00:46:40,740
as their reasoning and
their final answer,

1049
00:46:40,740 --> 00:46:42,240
then you can kind
of see, oh, hey,

1050
00:46:42,240 --> 00:46:43,470
this is where they
messed up, this

1051
00:46:43,470 --> 00:46:45,070
is where they got
something incorrect.

1052
00:46:45,070 --> 00:46:48,150
And so we're able to break down
the errors of chain of thought

1053
00:46:48,150 --> 00:46:51,180
into these different categories
that helps us better pinpoint,

1054
00:46:51,180 --> 00:46:52,810
why is it doing
this incorrectly,

1055
00:46:52,810 --> 00:46:57,230
how can we directly
target these issues.

1056
00:46:57,230 --> 00:46:59,270
And so currently,
chain of thought

1057
00:46:59,270 --> 00:47:02,640
works really effectively for
models of approximately 100

1058
00:47:02,640 --> 00:47:03,930
billion parameters or more.

1059
00:47:03,930 --> 00:47:04,940
Obviously, very big.

1060
00:47:04,940 --> 00:47:07,850

1061
00:47:07,850 --> 00:47:09,560
And so why is that?

1062
00:47:09,560 --> 00:47:13,580
An initial paper found that
one step missing and semantic

1063
00:47:13,580 --> 00:47:15,170
understanding chain
of thought errors

1064
00:47:15,170 --> 00:47:17,830
are the most common
among smaller models.

1065
00:47:17,830 --> 00:47:19,850
So you can think
of, oh, I forgot

1066
00:47:19,850 --> 00:47:21,585
to do this step in
the multiplication.

1067
00:47:21,585 --> 00:47:23,960
Or I actually don't really
understand what multiplication

1068
00:47:23,960 --> 00:47:25,100
is to begin with.

1069
00:47:25,100 --> 00:47:28,790
And so some potential reasons is
that maybe smaller models fail

1070
00:47:28,790 --> 00:47:31,580
at even relatively easy
simple mapping tasks,

1071
00:47:31,580 --> 00:47:34,550
they seem to have inherently
weaker arithmetic abilities,

1072
00:47:34,550 --> 00:47:36,800
and maybe they have
logical loopholes

1073
00:47:36,800 --> 00:47:39,840
and don't end up coming
at a final answer.

1074
00:47:39,840 --> 00:47:42,200
So all your reasoning is
correct, but for some reason,

1075
00:47:42,200 --> 00:47:44,600
you just couldn't
get quite there.

1076
00:47:44,600 --> 00:47:46,310
And so an interesting
line of research

1077
00:47:46,310 --> 00:47:48,890
would be to improve chain of
thought for smaller models,

1078
00:47:48,890 --> 00:47:55,790
and similarly allow more people
to work on interesting problems.

1079
00:47:55,790 --> 00:47:59,640
And so how could we
potentially do that?

1080
00:47:59,640 --> 00:48:03,450
Well, one idea is to generalize
this chain of thought reasoning.

1081
00:48:03,450 --> 00:48:06,968
So it's not necessarily that
we reason in all the same ways.

1082
00:48:06,968 --> 00:48:09,260
Like there are multiple ways
to think through a problem

1083
00:48:09,260 --> 00:48:11,380
rather than breaking
it down step by step.

1084
00:48:11,380 --> 00:48:16,490
And so we can perhaps
generalize chain of thought

1085
00:48:16,490 --> 00:48:18,700
to be more flexible
in different ways.

1086
00:48:18,700 --> 00:48:21,590

1087
00:48:21,590 --> 00:48:25,790
One example is this
tree of thoughts idea.

1088
00:48:25,790 --> 00:48:27,710
And so tree of
thoughts is considering

1089
00:48:27,710 --> 00:48:29,600
multiple different
reasoning paths,

1090
00:48:29,600 --> 00:48:32,090
and evaluating their
choices to decide

1091
00:48:32,090 --> 00:48:33,480
the next course of action.

1092
00:48:33,480 --> 00:48:35,540
And so this is
similar to the idea

1093
00:48:35,540 --> 00:48:37,580
that we can look ahead
and go backwards,

1094
00:48:37,580 --> 00:48:41,210
similar to a lot of the model
architectures that we've seen.

1095
00:48:41,210 --> 00:48:44,030
And so just having
multiple options

1096
00:48:44,030 --> 00:48:47,570
and being able to come out
with some more accurate output

1097
00:48:47,570 --> 00:48:50,190
at the end.

1098
00:48:50,190 --> 00:48:52,620
Another idea is
Socratic questioning.

1099
00:48:52,620 --> 00:48:56,670
So the idea that we are
dividing and conquering

1100
00:48:56,670 --> 00:48:58,740
in order to have this
self-questioning,

1101
00:48:58,740 --> 00:49:04,240
self-reflection idea
that Steven touched upon.

1102
00:49:04,240 --> 00:49:06,570
So the idea is a
self-questioning module

1103
00:49:06,570 --> 00:49:08,670
using a large scale
language model

1104
00:49:08,670 --> 00:49:11,310
to propose these
subproblems related

1105
00:49:11,310 --> 00:49:14,310
to the original problem
that recursively backtracks

1106
00:49:14,310 --> 00:49:17,020
and answers the subproblem
to the original problem.

1107
00:49:17,020 --> 00:49:18,983
So this is similar
to that initial idea

1108
00:49:18,983 --> 00:49:21,150
of chain of thought, except
rather than spelling out

1109
00:49:21,150 --> 00:49:22,800
all the steps for you.

1110
00:49:22,800 --> 00:49:25,500
The language model
reflects on how

1111
00:49:25,500 --> 00:49:26,940
can it break down
these problems,

1112
00:49:26,940 --> 00:49:29,780
how can it answer these problems
and get to the final answer.

1113
00:49:29,780 --> 00:49:34,360

1114
00:49:34,360 --> 00:49:35,890
Cool.

1115
00:49:35,890 --> 00:49:37,130
OK, let's see.

1116
00:49:37,130 --> 00:49:40,870
So let's go to some of the
more interesting topics

1117
00:49:40,870 --> 00:49:44,110
that are starting to become
relevant, especially in 2024.

1118
00:49:44,110 --> 00:49:47,350
So last year, we saw a big
explosion in language models,

1119
00:49:47,350 --> 00:49:50,530
especially with GPT-4 that
came out almost a year ago now.

1120
00:49:50,530 --> 00:49:53,650
And now what's happening is
we are starting to transition

1121
00:49:53,650 --> 00:49:55,370
towards more AI agents.

1122
00:49:55,370 --> 00:49:58,840
And it's very interesting to
see what differentiates an agent

1123
00:49:58,840 --> 00:50:01,660
from something like a model.

1124
00:50:01,660 --> 00:50:04,450
So I probably talk about a
bunch of different things,

1125
00:50:04,450 --> 00:50:08,080
such as actions, long-term
memory, communication,

1126
00:50:08,080 --> 00:50:09,580
bunch of stuff.

1127
00:50:09,580 --> 00:50:12,925
But let's start by, why
should we go and build agents?

1128
00:50:12,925 --> 00:50:15,790

1129
00:50:15,790 --> 00:50:17,350
Think about that.

1130
00:50:17,350 --> 00:50:22,120
So one key hypothesis
or I will say here is--

1131
00:50:22,120 --> 00:50:24,730
what's going to happen is
humans will communicate

1132
00:50:24,730 --> 00:50:28,150
with AI using natural
language, and AI

1133
00:50:28,150 --> 00:50:30,220
will be operating our machines.

1134
00:50:30,220 --> 00:50:33,860
Thus allowing for more intuitive
and efficient operations.

1135
00:50:33,860 --> 00:50:35,630
So if you think about a laptop.

1136
00:50:35,630 --> 00:50:40,100
If you show a laptop to
someone who's maybe a kid,

1137
00:50:40,100 --> 00:50:43,560
or who has never used a computer
before, they'll be like, OK,

1138
00:50:43,560 --> 00:50:44,810
why do I have to use this box?

1139
00:50:44,810 --> 00:50:46,537
Why can't I just talk to it?

1140
00:50:46,537 --> 00:50:47,870
Why can't it be more human-like?

1141
00:50:47,870 --> 00:50:49,870
I can just ask you to do
things and you can just

1142
00:50:49,870 --> 00:50:50,820
go do my work for me.

1143
00:50:50,820 --> 00:50:55,683
And that seems to be the
more human-like interface

1144
00:50:55,683 --> 00:50:56,850
to how things should happen.

1145
00:50:56,850 --> 00:50:59,392
And I think that's the way the
world will transition towards,

1146
00:50:59,392 --> 00:51:01,700
where instead of us
clicking or typing,

1147
00:51:01,700 --> 00:51:05,120
we talk to an AI using
natural language how

1148
00:51:05,120 --> 00:51:09,407
you talk to a human, and the
AI will go and do your work.

1149
00:51:09,407 --> 00:51:10,990
Actually, I have a
blog on this, which

1150
00:51:10,990 --> 00:51:14,200
is called Software 3.0 if
you want to check that out.

1151
00:51:14,200 --> 00:51:15,770
But yeah, cool.

1152
00:51:15,770 --> 00:51:19,270
So for agents, why?

1153
00:51:19,270 --> 00:51:21,590
Do you want agents?

1154
00:51:21,590 --> 00:51:25,630
So as it turns out, a single
call to a larger foundation AI

1155
00:51:25,630 --> 00:51:27,970
model is usually not enough.

1156
00:51:27,970 --> 00:51:30,470
You can do a lot more
by building systems.

1157
00:51:30,470 --> 00:51:33,610
And by systems, you mean
doing more things, like model

1158
00:51:33,610 --> 00:51:37,180
chaining, model reflection,
other mechanisms.

1159
00:51:37,180 --> 00:51:39,200
And this requires a
lot of different stuff.

1160
00:51:39,200 --> 00:51:42,950
So you require memory, you
require large context lengths,

1161
00:51:42,950 --> 00:51:44,480
you also want to
do personalization,

1162
00:51:44,480 --> 00:51:45,897
you want to be
able to do actions,

1163
00:51:45,897 --> 00:51:49,508
you want to be able
to do internet access.

1164
00:51:49,508 --> 00:51:51,550
And then you can accomplish
a lot of these things

1165
00:51:51,550 --> 00:51:53,710
with this kind of agents.

1166
00:51:53,710 --> 00:51:56,740
Here's a diagram breaking
down the different parts

1167
00:51:56,740 --> 00:51:57,880
of the agents.

1168
00:51:57,880 --> 00:51:59,420
This is from Lillian Weng.

1169
00:51:59,420 --> 00:52:02,200
She's a senior
researcher at OpenAI.

1170
00:52:02,200 --> 00:52:04,480
So if you want to build
really powerful agents,

1171
00:52:04,480 --> 00:52:07,120
you need to really just think
of that as you're building this

1172
00:52:07,120 --> 00:52:09,380
a new kind of
computer, which has

1173
00:52:09,380 --> 00:52:11,790
all this different
ingredients to build.

1174
00:52:11,790 --> 00:52:13,413
You have to build
memory, and you

1175
00:52:13,413 --> 00:52:15,080
have to think about
memory from scratch.

1176
00:52:15,080 --> 00:52:16,170
How do you do long-term memory?

1177
00:52:16,170 --> 00:52:17,503
How do you do short-term memory?

1178
00:52:17,503 --> 00:52:18,840
How do you do planning?

1179
00:52:18,840 --> 00:52:20,690
How do we think
about reflection?

1180
00:52:20,690 --> 00:52:23,030
If something goes wrong,
how do you correct that?

1181
00:52:23,030 --> 00:52:24,810
How do you have
chain of thoughts?

1182
00:52:24,810 --> 00:52:26,275
How do you decompose a goal?

1183
00:52:26,275 --> 00:52:28,400
So if I say something like
book me a trip to Italy,

1184
00:52:28,400 --> 00:52:31,130
for example, how do you
break that down to sub goals,

1185
00:52:31,130 --> 00:52:32,840
for example, for the agent?

1186
00:52:32,840 --> 00:52:35,900
And also being able to take
all this planning and all

1187
00:52:35,900 --> 00:52:38,305
this stuffs into actual action.

1188
00:52:38,305 --> 00:52:39,680
So that becomes
really important.

1189
00:52:39,680 --> 00:52:42,510
And enable all of
that using tool use.

1190
00:52:42,510 --> 00:52:45,710
So if you have say like
calculators, or calendars,

1191
00:52:45,710 --> 00:52:47,942
or code interpreters, and so on.

1192
00:52:47,942 --> 00:52:50,150
So you want to be able to
utilize existing tools that

1193
00:52:50,150 --> 00:52:51,030
are out there.

1194
00:52:51,030 --> 00:52:55,070
This is similar to how we as
a human, use a calculator,

1195
00:52:55,070 --> 00:52:55,770
for example.

1196
00:52:55,770 --> 00:52:58,580
So we also want AI to be
able to use existing tools

1197
00:52:58,580 --> 00:53:00,745
and become more
efficient and powerful.

1198
00:53:00,745 --> 00:53:03,450

1199
00:53:03,450 --> 00:53:05,345
This was actually
one of the demos.

1200
00:53:05,345 --> 00:53:06,720
This is actually
from my company.

1201
00:53:06,720 --> 00:53:08,940
But this was one of the
first demonstrations

1202
00:53:08,940 --> 00:53:11,130
of agents in the real
world, where we actually

1203
00:53:11,130 --> 00:53:14,400
had it passed the online
driving test in California.

1204
00:53:14,400 --> 00:53:17,940
So this was actually like a live
exam we took as a demonstration.

1205
00:53:17,940 --> 00:53:21,545
So this was a friends
or a driving test,

1206
00:53:21,545 --> 00:53:23,170
which can actually
take from your home.

1207
00:53:23,170 --> 00:53:27,510
And so the person had their
hands up above the keyboard,

1208
00:53:27,510 --> 00:53:29,700
and they were being
recorded on the webcam.

1209
00:53:29,700 --> 00:53:31,540
There was also like
a screen recorded.

1210
00:53:31,540 --> 00:53:33,737
And the DMV actually
had the person installed

1211
00:53:33,737 --> 00:53:35,820
like a special software
in the computer to detect,

1212
00:53:35,820 --> 00:53:36,917
it's not a bot.

1213
00:53:36,917 --> 00:53:39,000
But still the agent could
actually go and complete

1214
00:53:39,000 --> 00:53:39,500
the exam.

1215
00:53:39,500 --> 00:53:41,590
So that was interesting to see.

1216
00:53:41,590 --> 00:53:42,870
[LAUGHS]

1217
00:53:42,870 --> 00:53:44,640
So we set the
record in this case

1218
00:53:44,640 --> 00:53:46,800
to be the first
AI to actually get

1219
00:53:46,800 --> 00:53:49,020
a driving permit in California.

1220
00:53:49,020 --> 00:53:52,110
And this is the agent actually
going and doing things.

1221
00:53:52,110 --> 00:53:55,020
So here the person
has their hands just

1222
00:53:55,020 --> 00:53:58,050
above the keyboard
for the webcam.

1223
00:53:58,050 --> 00:54:00,300
And the agent is
running on the laptop

1224
00:54:00,300 --> 00:54:02,930
and it's answering
all the questions.

1225
00:54:02,930 --> 00:54:05,360
So all of this is happening
autonomously in this case.

1226
00:54:05,360 --> 00:54:07,460
And so this was roughly
around 40 questions.

1227
00:54:07,460 --> 00:54:09,460
The agent maybe made like
two or three mistakes,

1228
00:54:09,460 --> 00:54:10,835
but was able to
successfully pass

1229
00:54:10,835 --> 00:54:12,900
the whole test in this case.

1230
00:54:12,900 --> 00:54:15,340
So this was really fun.

1231
00:54:15,340 --> 00:54:17,020
Let me go to the end.

1232
00:54:17,020 --> 00:54:18,101
Yeah.

1233
00:54:18,101 --> 00:54:21,880
[LAUGHS]

1234
00:54:21,880 --> 00:54:22,380
So yeah.

1235
00:54:22,380 --> 00:54:24,200
So you can imagine,
there's a lot of fun things

1236
00:54:24,200 --> 00:54:25,367
that can happen with agents.

1237
00:54:25,367 --> 00:54:27,300
This was actually
a tech attempt.

1238
00:54:27,300 --> 00:54:29,570
So we informed the DMV
after we took the exam.

1239
00:54:29,570 --> 00:54:31,412
So this was really funny.

1240
00:54:31,412 --> 00:54:33,620
But you can imagine, there's
so many different things

1241
00:54:33,620 --> 00:54:37,910
you can enable once you have
this capabilities that are

1242
00:54:37,910 --> 00:54:42,750
available for everyone to use.

1243
00:54:42,750 --> 00:54:46,590
And this becomes a
question of, why should we

1244
00:54:46,590 --> 00:54:48,810
build more human-like agents?

1245
00:54:48,810 --> 00:54:51,450
And I would say this
is very interesting

1246
00:54:51,450 --> 00:54:53,520
because it's almost like
saying, why should we

1247
00:54:53,520 --> 00:54:55,350
build humanoid robots?

1248
00:54:55,350 --> 00:54:57,820
Why can't we just build a
different kind of robot?

1249
00:54:57,820 --> 00:54:59,170
Why do you want humanoid robots?

1250
00:54:59,170 --> 00:55:01,170
And it's similar question
here, why do you want,

1251
00:55:01,170 --> 00:55:03,030
human-like agents?

1252
00:55:03,030 --> 00:55:05,190
And obviously, this
is very interesting

1253
00:55:05,190 --> 00:55:09,190
because a lot of the technology
website is built for humans.

1254
00:55:09,190 --> 00:55:11,190
And then we can go and
reuse that infrastructure

1255
00:55:11,190 --> 00:55:12,550
instead of building new things.

1256
00:55:12,550 --> 00:55:14,050
And so that becomes
very interesting

1257
00:55:14,050 --> 00:55:16,680
because you can just deploy
these agents using the existing

1258
00:55:16,680 --> 00:55:17,755
technology.

1259
00:55:17,755 --> 00:55:20,130
Second is you can imagine this
agents could become almost

1260
00:55:20,130 --> 00:55:21,463
like a digital extension of you.

1261
00:55:21,463 --> 00:55:23,963
So they can learn about you,
they can know your preferences,

1262
00:55:23,963 --> 00:55:26,010
they can know what you
like, what you don't like,

1263
00:55:26,010 --> 00:55:28,680
and be able to act
on your behalf.

1264
00:55:28,680 --> 00:55:31,050
They also have very less
restrictive boundaries,

1265
00:55:31,050 --> 00:55:33,500
so they're able to handle
say, like logins, payments,

1266
00:55:33,500 --> 00:55:35,910
and so on, which might be
harder with things like API

1267
00:55:35,910 --> 00:55:36,720
for example.

1268
00:55:36,720 --> 00:55:39,960
But this is easier to do if you
are doing more computer-based

1269
00:55:39,960 --> 00:55:43,240
control, like a human.

1270
00:55:43,240 --> 00:55:45,730
You can imagine the problem
is also fundamentally simpler,

1271
00:55:45,730 --> 00:55:49,270
because you just have an action
space like clicking and typing,

1272
00:55:49,270 --> 00:55:52,390
which itself is a fundamentally
limited action space.

1273
00:55:52,390 --> 00:55:54,832
So that's a simpler
problem to solve

1274
00:55:54,832 --> 00:55:56,290
rather than building
something that

1275
00:55:56,290 --> 00:56:01,380
is maybe more general purpose.

1276
00:56:01,380 --> 00:56:03,840
And another interesting
thing about this kind

1277
00:56:03,840 --> 00:56:06,070
of human-like agents is
you can also teach them.

1278
00:56:06,070 --> 00:56:07,990
So you can teach them
how you will do things,

1279
00:56:07,990 --> 00:56:09,490
they can maybe
record you passively.

1280
00:56:09,490 --> 00:56:11,640
And they can learn from
you and then improve.

1281
00:56:11,640 --> 00:56:13,830
And this is also becomes
an interesting way

1282
00:56:13,830 --> 00:56:15,455
to improve this
agents over time.

1283
00:56:15,455 --> 00:56:18,260

1284
00:56:18,260 --> 00:56:20,540
So when we talk
about agents, there's

1285
00:56:20,540 --> 00:56:22,280
this map that
people like to use,

1286
00:56:22,280 --> 00:56:25,100
which is called the 5
different levels of autonomy.

1287
00:56:25,100 --> 00:56:27,090
This actually came
from self-driving cars.

1288
00:56:27,090 --> 00:56:31,630
So how this works is
you have L0 to L5.

1289
00:56:31,630 --> 00:56:34,910
So L0 to L2 is the
parts of autonomy

1290
00:56:34,910 --> 00:56:36,430
where the human is in control.

1291
00:56:36,430 --> 00:56:38,750
So here is the human
is driving the car.

1292
00:56:38,750 --> 00:56:41,670
And there might be some partial
automation that's happening,

1293
00:56:41,670 --> 00:56:46,760
which could be some
auto assist features.

1294
00:56:46,760 --> 00:56:49,280
This starts becoming interesting
when you have something L3.

1295
00:56:49,280 --> 00:56:52,550
So in L3, you still
have a human in the car,

1296
00:56:52,550 --> 00:56:57,800
but most of the time, the car is
able to drive itself on highways

1297
00:56:57,800 --> 00:56:59,780
or most of the roads.

1298
00:56:59,780 --> 00:57:02,960
L4 is you still have
a human, but the car

1299
00:57:02,960 --> 00:57:04,850
is doing all the driving.

1300
00:57:04,850 --> 00:57:08,270
And this is maybe if you have
driven a Tesla on autopilot

1301
00:57:08,270 --> 00:57:11,630
before, that's like an
L4 autonomous vehicle.

1302
00:57:11,630 --> 00:57:14,990
And L5 is basically you don't
have a driver in the car.

1303
00:57:14,990 --> 00:57:19,630
So the car is able to go and
handle all parts of the system,

1304
00:57:19,630 --> 00:57:20,680
there's no fallback.

1305
00:57:20,680 --> 00:57:22,870
And this is what like
Waymo is doing right now.

1306
00:57:22,870 --> 00:57:28,590
So if you take a self-driving,
if you sit-in a Waymo in SF,

1307
00:57:28,590 --> 00:57:30,330
then you can experience
a L5 autonomy

1308
00:57:30,330 --> 00:57:32,820
car, where there's
no human and the AI

1309
00:57:32,820 --> 00:57:34,320
is driving the whole car itself.

1310
00:57:34,320 --> 00:57:37,210
And so same thing also
applies for agents.

1311
00:57:37,210 --> 00:57:40,140
So you can almost imagine if
you are building something

1312
00:57:40,140 --> 00:57:42,053
like an L4 level
capability, that's

1313
00:57:42,053 --> 00:57:43,470
where a human is
still in the loop

1314
00:57:43,470 --> 00:57:45,137
and showing that
nothing is going wrong.

1315
00:57:45,137 --> 00:57:48,000
And so this is you still
have some bottlenecks.

1316
00:57:48,000 --> 00:57:50,988
But if you are able to reach
L5 level of autonomy on agents.

1317
00:57:50,988 --> 00:57:53,280
And that's basically saying
if you ask an agent to book

1318
00:57:53,280 --> 00:57:54,430
a flight and that happens.

1319
00:57:54,430 --> 00:57:57,280
You ask it for maybe like,
go, maybe order this for me.

1320
00:57:57,280 --> 00:58:01,320
Or maybe go whatever
things you care about.

1321
00:58:01,320 --> 00:58:03,560
And that can all
happen autonomously.

1322
00:58:03,560 --> 00:58:06,060
So that's where things start
to become very interesting when

1323
00:58:06,060 --> 00:58:08,220
we can start reaching
from L4 to L5

1324
00:58:08,220 --> 00:58:12,580
and don't even need a
human in the loop anymore.

1325
00:58:12,580 --> 00:58:15,170
Cool.

1326
00:58:15,170 --> 00:58:17,140
So when you think
about building agents,

1327
00:58:17,140 --> 00:58:19,780
this predominantly two routes.

1328
00:58:19,780 --> 00:58:25,000
So the first one is API, where
you can go and control anything

1329
00:58:25,000 --> 00:58:27,270
based on APIs that are
available out there.

1330
00:58:27,270 --> 00:58:30,490
So OpenAI has been trying
this with ChatGPT Plugins,

1331
00:58:30,490 --> 00:58:31,460
for example.

1332
00:58:31,460 --> 00:58:34,662
There's also a bunch
of work from Berkeley.

1333
00:58:34,662 --> 00:58:36,370
So Berkeley had this
work called Gorilla,

1334
00:58:36,370 --> 00:58:39,580
where you could train a
foundation model to control

1335
00:58:39,580 --> 00:58:41,560
10,000 APIs.

1336
00:58:41,560 --> 00:58:44,573
And there's a lot of interesting
stuff happening here.

1337
00:58:44,573 --> 00:58:46,990
A second direction of work is
more like direct interaction

1338
00:58:46,990 --> 00:58:48,130
with the computer.

1339
00:58:48,130 --> 00:58:50,720
And there's different
companies trying this out.

1340
00:58:50,720 --> 00:58:51,890
So we have one of that.

1341
00:58:51,890 --> 00:58:53,980
There's also this
startup called Adept

1342
00:58:53,980 --> 00:58:56,210
which is trying this
human-like interaction.

1343
00:58:56,210 --> 00:59:00,690

1344
00:59:00,690 --> 00:59:02,240
Yeah, maybe I can
show this thing.

1345
00:59:02,240 --> 00:59:07,380
So this is an idea of what you
can enable by having agents.

1346
00:59:07,380 --> 00:59:11,100
So what we are doing here is--

1347
00:59:11,100 --> 00:59:13,110
here we have our
agent and we told

1348
00:59:13,110 --> 00:59:16,980
it to go to Twitter
and make a post.

1349
00:59:16,980 --> 00:59:20,340
And so it's going and
controlling the computer

1350
00:59:20,340 --> 00:59:21,840
during this whole interaction.

1351
00:59:21,840 --> 00:59:28,710
And once it's done, it can
send me a response back,

1352
00:59:28,710 --> 00:59:29,920
which you can see here.

1353
00:59:29,920 --> 00:59:32,430
And so this becomes interesting
because you don't really

1354
00:59:32,430 --> 00:59:34,410
need APIs if you have
this kind of agents.

1355
00:59:34,410 --> 00:59:36,660
So if I have an agent that
can go control my computer,

1356
00:59:36,660 --> 00:59:38,580
can go control websites,
can do whatever

1357
00:59:38,580 --> 00:59:41,280
it wants, almost in a human-like
manner, like what you can do,

1358
00:59:41,280 --> 00:59:43,170
then you don't really
need APIs because this

1359
00:59:43,170 --> 00:59:48,930
becomes like the abstraction
layer to allow any control.

1360
00:59:48,930 --> 00:59:50,550
So it's going to be
really fascinating

1361
00:59:50,550 --> 00:59:53,470
once we have this kind of agent
start to work in the real world,

1362
00:59:53,470 --> 00:59:56,260
and a lot of transitions
we'll see in technology.

1363
00:59:56,260 --> 01:00:06,290

1364
01:00:06,290 --> 01:00:06,890
Cool.

1365
01:00:06,890 --> 01:00:11,070
OK, so let's move on to the next
topic when it comes to agents.

1366
01:00:11,070 --> 01:00:15,590
So one very interesting
thing here is memory.

1367
01:00:15,590 --> 01:00:19,280
So, yeah, so we'll say a good
way to think about a model

1368
01:00:19,280 --> 01:00:21,930
is almost think of it
like a compute chip.

1369
01:00:21,930 --> 01:00:24,890
So what happens is you have
some sort of input tokens, which

1370
01:00:24,890 --> 01:00:27,980
are defined in natural language,
which are going as the input

1371
01:00:27,980 --> 01:00:28,610
to a model.

1372
01:00:28,610 --> 01:00:30,590
And then you get some
output tokens out.

1373
01:00:30,590 --> 01:00:33,470
And the output tokens are,
again, natural language.

1374
01:00:33,470 --> 01:00:36,080
And if you have
something like a GPT-3.5,

1375
01:00:36,080 --> 01:00:39,200
that used to be something
like a 8,000 length token.

1376
01:00:39,200 --> 01:00:44,000
With GPT-4, this became like a
16,000 [INAUDIBLE],, one 28,000.

1377
01:00:44,000 --> 01:00:46,790
So you can almost imagine
this as the token size

1378
01:00:46,790 --> 01:00:49,790
or the instruction size of
this compute unit, which

1379
01:00:49,790 --> 01:00:54,830
is powered by a neural
network in this case.

1380
01:00:54,830 --> 01:00:57,530
And so this is basically what
GPT-4, you can imagine it.

1381
01:00:57,530 --> 01:01:01,280
It's almost like a CPU and
it's taking some input tokens,

1382
01:01:01,280 --> 01:01:03,170
defined a natural
language, doing

1383
01:01:03,170 --> 01:01:05,420
some computation over them,
transforming those tokens

1384
01:01:05,420 --> 01:01:07,910
and giving out
some output tokens.

1385
01:01:07,910 --> 01:01:10,700
This is actually similar to how
you think about memory chips,

1386
01:01:10,700 --> 01:01:11,560
for example.

1387
01:01:11,560 --> 01:01:15,670
So here I'm showing
a MIPS32 processor.

1388
01:01:15,670 --> 01:01:17,670
That's one of the earliest
processors out there.

1389
01:01:17,670 --> 01:01:20,390
And so what it's doing is you
have input tokens and output

1390
01:01:20,390 --> 01:01:22,320
tokens in binary,
like 0s and 1s.

1391
01:01:22,320 --> 01:01:23,360
But [INAUDIBLE] of
that, you can imagine

1392
01:01:23,360 --> 01:01:24,735
we are doing very
similar things,

1393
01:01:24,735 --> 01:01:27,350
but just over
natural language now.

1394
01:01:27,350 --> 01:01:30,992
And now, if you think
more about this analogy.

1395
01:01:30,992 --> 01:01:32,450
So you can start
thinking, OK, what

1396
01:01:32,450 --> 01:01:34,190
we want to do is
take whatever we have

1397
01:01:34,190 --> 01:01:37,160
been doing in building
computers, and CPUs, and logic,

1398
01:01:37,160 --> 01:01:37,830
and so on.

1399
01:01:37,830 --> 01:01:40,430
But can we generalize all
of that to natural language?

1400
01:01:40,430 --> 01:01:43,430
So you can start thinking about,
how current processors work?

1401
01:01:43,430 --> 01:01:45,660
How current computer chips work?

1402
01:01:45,660 --> 01:01:50,660
You have instructions, you have
memory, you have variables.

1403
01:01:50,660 --> 01:01:54,470
And then you run this
over and over to each line

1404
01:01:54,470 --> 01:01:58,895
of binary sequence of
instructions to output code.

1405
01:01:58,895 --> 01:02:01,520
And you can start thinking about
transformers in a similar way,

1406
01:02:01,520 --> 01:02:03,440
where you can have
the transformer acting

1407
01:02:03,440 --> 01:02:05,180
as a compute unit,
you are passing

1408
01:02:05,180 --> 01:02:07,020
it some sort of
instructions line by line.

1409
01:02:07,020 --> 01:02:10,682
And each instruction can
contain some primitives

1410
01:02:10,682 --> 01:02:12,140
which are defining
like what to do,

1411
01:02:12,140 --> 01:02:13,460
which could be the user command.

1412
01:02:13,460 --> 01:02:15,043
It could have some
memory parts, which

1413
01:02:15,043 --> 01:02:17,622
are retrieved from external,
like a disk, which in this case

1414
01:02:17,622 --> 01:02:19,830
could be like something like
a personalization system

1415
01:02:19,830 --> 01:02:22,050
and so on, as well as
some sort of variables.

1416
01:02:22,050 --> 01:02:24,467
And then you're taking this
and running this line by line.

1417
01:02:24,467 --> 01:02:28,268
And that's a pretty good way
to think about what something

1418
01:02:28,268 --> 01:02:29,310
like this could be doing.

1419
01:02:29,310 --> 01:02:31,685
And you can almost imagine
there could be new programming

1420
01:02:31,685 --> 01:02:35,270
languages you can build, which
are specific to programming

1421
01:02:35,270 --> 01:02:36,826
like transformers.

1422
01:02:36,826 --> 01:02:39,450

1423
01:02:39,450 --> 01:02:41,970
And so when it comes to
memory, traditionally,

1424
01:02:41,970 --> 01:02:44,590
how we think about
memory is like a disk.

1425
01:02:44,590 --> 01:02:46,330
So it's like long-lived,
it's persistent.

1426
01:02:46,330 --> 01:02:49,080
When the computer shuts
down, you save all your data

1427
01:02:49,080 --> 01:02:52,050
from the RAM to the disk,
and then you can persist it

1428
01:02:52,050 --> 01:02:55,080
and then you can load
it back when you want.

1429
01:02:55,080 --> 01:02:58,200
You want to enable something
very similar when you have AI

1430
01:02:58,200 --> 01:02:59,400
and you have agents.

1431
01:02:59,400 --> 01:03:01,110
And so you want
to have mechanisms

1432
01:03:01,110 --> 01:03:04,240
where I can store this data,
and then retrieve this data.

1433
01:03:04,240 --> 01:03:07,590
And right now how we're doing
this is like through embeddings.

1434
01:03:07,590 --> 01:03:13,980
So you can take any PDF or
any modality you care about,

1435
01:03:13,980 --> 01:03:17,280
convert that to embeddings
using an embedding model.

1436
01:03:17,280 --> 01:03:19,890
And store that embedding
in a vector database.

1437
01:03:19,890 --> 01:03:24,870
And later when we actually care
about doing any sort of access

1438
01:03:24,870 --> 01:03:27,630
to the memory, you can load the
relevant part of the embedding,

1439
01:03:27,630 --> 01:03:29,610
put that in as part
of your instruction

1440
01:03:29,610 --> 01:03:31,570
and feed that to the model.

1441
01:03:31,570 --> 01:03:34,530
And so this is how we
are thinking about memory

1442
01:03:34,530 --> 01:03:36,120
with AI, this space.

1443
01:03:36,120 --> 01:03:38,440
And then you essentially
have the retrieval models,

1444
01:03:38,440 --> 01:03:41,770
which are acting as a function
to store and retrieve memory.

1445
01:03:41,770 --> 01:03:43,900
And the embeddings becomes
the layer of the-- it's

1446
01:03:43,900 --> 01:03:47,770
basically the format
you're using to encode

1447
01:03:47,770 --> 01:03:49,258
the memory in this case.

1448
01:03:49,258 --> 01:03:50,800
There's still a lot
of open questions

1449
01:03:50,800 --> 01:03:55,130
because how this works right
now is you just do simple k-NN.

1450
01:03:55,130 --> 01:03:57,550
So it's very simple nearest
neighbor search, which

1451
01:03:57,550 --> 01:03:59,540
is not efficient, it
doesn't really generalize,

1452
01:03:59,540 --> 01:04:00,470
doesn't scale.

1453
01:04:00,470 --> 01:04:01,887
And so there's a
lot of things you

1454
01:04:01,887 --> 01:04:05,380
can think about, especially
hierarchy, temporal coherence.

1455
01:04:05,380 --> 01:04:08,900
Because a lot of memory
data is like a time series.

1456
01:04:08,900 --> 01:04:10,760
So there's a lot
of temporal parts.

1457
01:04:10,760 --> 01:04:13,323
There's also a lot of structure,
usually in a lot of data.

1458
01:04:13,323 --> 01:04:15,490
You could use a structure,
it could be like a graph,

1459
01:04:15,490 --> 01:04:16,430
for example.

1460
01:04:16,430 --> 01:04:19,750
And there's also a lot of things
you can do on online adaptation.

1461
01:04:19,750 --> 01:04:21,852
Because like most
[? chair data ?] is not static.

1462
01:04:21,852 --> 01:04:23,810
You're always learning,
you're always adapting,

1463
01:04:23,810 --> 01:04:25,352
there's things
changing all the time.

1464
01:04:25,352 --> 01:04:28,252
And a good model over here is
maybe how the human brain works.

1465
01:04:28,252 --> 01:04:30,460
So if you think about
something like the hippocampus,

1466
01:04:30,460 --> 01:04:33,160
it's like people still don't
know how it fully works,

1467
01:04:33,160 --> 01:04:35,832
but it's something you're
learning new things on the fly,

1468
01:04:35,832 --> 01:04:38,290
you're creating new memories,
you're adapting new memories,

1469
01:04:38,290 --> 01:04:38,990
and so on.

1470
01:04:38,990 --> 01:04:40,948
And so I think it'll be
very fascinating to see

1471
01:04:40,948 --> 01:04:42,885
how this area of research
evolves over time.

1472
01:04:42,885 --> 01:04:46,425

1473
01:04:46,425 --> 01:04:48,550
Similarly, like a very
relevant problem with memory

1474
01:04:48,550 --> 01:04:51,205
is personalization.

1475
01:04:51,205 --> 01:04:53,830
Suppose now you have this agents
that are doing things for you.

1476
01:04:53,830 --> 01:04:55,930
Then you want to make sure that
the agent actually knows what

1477
01:04:55,930 --> 01:04:57,310
you like, What you don't like.

1478
01:04:57,310 --> 01:04:59,800
Suppose you tell an agent to
go book your $1,000 flight,

1479
01:04:59,800 --> 01:05:03,310
but maybe it books you the wrong
flight and just wastes a lot

1480
01:05:03,310 --> 01:05:04,160
of your money.

1481
01:05:04,160 --> 01:05:08,390
Or maybe it does a lot of wrong
actions, which is not good.

1482
01:05:08,390 --> 01:05:11,890
So you want the agent
to learn about what

1483
01:05:11,890 --> 01:05:14,840
you like and understand that.

1484
01:05:14,840 --> 01:05:17,720
And this becomes about
forming a long-lived user

1485
01:05:17,720 --> 01:05:20,180
memory, for example, where
the more you interact with it,

1486
01:05:20,180 --> 01:05:22,310
the more it should
form a memory about you

1487
01:05:22,310 --> 01:05:25,220
and be able to use that.

1488
01:05:25,220 --> 01:05:26,715
This could have
different flavors.

1489
01:05:26,715 --> 01:05:29,090
So one could be like explicit
where you can tell it like,

1490
01:05:29,090 --> 01:05:31,895
OK, here's my allergies,
here's my flight preferences,

1491
01:05:31,895 --> 01:05:33,638
I like window
versus aisle seats,

1492
01:05:33,638 --> 01:05:35,180
here's my favorite
dishes, and so on.

1493
01:05:35,180 --> 01:05:37,820
But this could also be
implicit, where it could be say,

1494
01:05:37,820 --> 01:05:39,470
maybe I like Adidas over Nike.

1495
01:05:39,470 --> 01:05:43,793
Or if I'm on Amazon and if I
have this 10 different shirts I

1496
01:05:43,793 --> 01:05:45,710
can buy, maybe I will
buy this particular type

1497
01:05:45,710 --> 01:05:47,010
of shirt and brand and so on.

1498
01:05:47,010 --> 01:05:49,427
And so there's also a lot of
implicit learning you can do,

1499
01:05:49,427 --> 01:05:54,810
which is more based on
feedback or comparisons.

1500
01:05:54,810 --> 01:05:56,800
And there's a lot of
challenges involved here.

1501
01:05:56,800 --> 01:05:59,110
So you can imagine, it's how
do you collect this data?

1502
01:05:59,110 --> 01:06:00,400
How do you form this memory?

1503
01:06:00,400 --> 01:06:02,430
How do you learn?

1504
01:06:02,430 --> 01:06:05,400
And do you use supervised
learning versus feedback?

1505
01:06:05,400 --> 01:06:08,203
How do you learn on the fly?

1506
01:06:08,203 --> 01:06:09,870
And while you are
doing all of this, how

1507
01:06:09,870 --> 01:06:11,950
do you preserve user privacy?

1508
01:06:11,950 --> 01:06:14,820
Because like for some,
a system to personalize.

1509
01:06:14,820 --> 01:06:16,622
It just needs to
know a lot about you.

1510
01:06:16,622 --> 01:06:18,330
But then how do you
ensure that if you're

1511
01:06:18,330 --> 01:06:20,910
building systems like that,
that this is actually safe?

1512
01:06:20,910 --> 01:06:25,220
And it's not violating
any of your privacy.

1513
01:06:25,220 --> 01:06:29,370

1514
01:06:29,370 --> 01:06:33,030
An interesting area
when it comes to agents

1515
01:06:33,030 --> 01:06:34,040
is also communication.

1516
01:06:34,040 --> 01:06:36,540
So now you can imagine, suppose
you have this one agent that

1517
01:06:36,540 --> 01:06:37,830
can go and do things for you.

1518
01:06:37,830 --> 01:06:40,190
But why not have
multiple agents?

1519
01:06:40,190 --> 01:06:43,052

1520
01:06:43,052 --> 01:06:44,760
If I have an agent
and you have an agent.

1521
01:06:44,760 --> 01:06:46,600
And this agent starts
communicating with each other.

1522
01:06:46,600 --> 01:06:48,600
And so I think we'll start
seeing this phenomena

1523
01:06:48,600 --> 01:06:51,510
where you will have multi-agent
autonomous systems, where

1524
01:06:51,510 --> 01:06:53,580
each agent can go and
do things, and then

1525
01:06:53,580 --> 01:06:55,950
that agent can go and talk
to other agents and so on.

1526
01:06:55,950 --> 01:06:58,500
And so that's going
to be fascinating.

1527
01:06:58,500 --> 01:07:01,620

1528
01:07:01,620 --> 01:07:03,040
Why do you want to do this?

1529
01:07:03,040 --> 01:07:06,270
So one is if you have a single
agent, it will always be slow.

1530
01:07:06,270 --> 01:07:08,298
It has to do everything
sequentially.

1531
01:07:08,298 --> 01:07:09,840
But if you have a
multi-agent system,

1532
01:07:09,840 --> 01:07:11,620
then you can
paralyze the system.

1533
01:07:11,620 --> 01:07:14,277
So instead of one agent,
I had thousands of agents.

1534
01:07:14,277 --> 01:07:16,110
Agent can go do something
for me in parallel

1535
01:07:16,110 --> 01:07:17,700
instead of just having one.

1536
01:07:17,700 --> 01:07:20,080
Second is you can also
have spatialized agents.

1537
01:07:20,080 --> 01:07:23,400
So I could have an agent that's
specifically for spreadsheets,

1538
01:07:23,400 --> 01:07:25,540
or I have an agent that
can operate my Slack,

1539
01:07:25,540 --> 01:07:27,540
I have an agent that can
operate my web browser.

1540
01:07:27,540 --> 01:07:29,260
And then I can route
to different agents

1541
01:07:29,260 --> 01:07:31,430
to different things
I want to do.

1542
01:07:31,430 --> 01:07:32,480
And that can help.

1543
01:07:32,480 --> 01:07:35,080
Like this is almost what
you do in a factory.

1544
01:07:35,080 --> 01:07:37,990
You have spatialized
workers, each worker

1545
01:07:37,990 --> 01:07:39,758
is doing something
they're specialized to.

1546
01:07:39,758 --> 01:07:41,800
And this actually is
something that we found over

1547
01:07:41,800 --> 01:07:44,380
like the period
of human history,

1548
01:07:44,380 --> 01:07:47,520
that this is the right way
to divide tasks and get

1549
01:07:47,520 --> 01:07:51,150
the maximum performance.

1550
01:07:51,150 --> 01:07:53,070
There's a lot of
challenges here, too.

1551
01:07:53,070 --> 01:07:55,522
The biggest one is just how
do you exchange information.

1552
01:07:55,522 --> 01:07:57,480
Because now what's
happening is like everything

1553
01:07:57,480 --> 01:07:58,938
is happening over
natural language.

1554
01:07:58,938 --> 01:08:01,660
And natural language
itself is lossy.

1555
01:08:01,660 --> 01:08:03,840
So it's very easy to have
miscommunication gaps.

1556
01:08:03,840 --> 01:08:05,640
Like even when humans
talk to each other,

1557
01:08:05,640 --> 01:08:07,920
there's a lot of
miscommunication.

1558
01:08:07,920 --> 01:08:10,590
You lose information a lot,
because natural language

1559
01:08:10,590 --> 01:08:11,530
is ambiguous.

1560
01:08:11,530 --> 01:08:14,130
So you just need to have better
protocols or a better ways

1561
01:08:14,130 --> 01:08:17,100
to ensure that if agents are
communicating with other agents,

1562
01:08:17,100 --> 01:08:20,100
it doesn't cause mistakes,
it doesn't lead to a havoc,

1563
01:08:20,100 --> 01:08:22,800
for example.

1564
01:08:22,800 --> 01:08:25,470
This can also lead to
building different interesting

1565
01:08:25,470 --> 01:08:26,640
primitives.

1566
01:08:26,640 --> 01:08:29,520
So here's one example primitive
you can think about is like,

1567
01:08:29,520 --> 01:08:31,710
suppose what if I
have a manager agent?

1568
01:08:31,710 --> 01:08:33,750
And the manager agent
can go and coordinate

1569
01:08:33,750 --> 01:08:35,080
a bunch of worker agents.

1570
01:08:35,080 --> 01:08:38,850
And this is very similar to a
human organization, for example.

1571
01:08:38,850 --> 01:08:42,210
So you can have this hierarchy
where like, OK, if I'm a user,

1572
01:08:42,210 --> 01:08:45,240
I'm talking to this one main
agent, but behind the scene,

1573
01:08:45,240 --> 01:08:47,819
this agent is going and talking
to its own worker agents

1574
01:08:47,819 --> 01:08:49,990
and showing that each worker
goes and does the task.

1575
01:08:49,990 --> 01:08:51,517
And once everything
is done, then

1576
01:08:51,517 --> 01:08:53,350
the manager agent comes
back to me and says,

1577
01:08:53,350 --> 01:08:54,478
OK, this thing is done.

1578
01:08:54,478 --> 01:08:56,020
And so you can
imagine, there's a lot

1579
01:08:56,020 --> 01:08:57,850
of this primitives
that can be built.

1580
01:08:57,850 --> 01:09:00,010
A good way to also think
about this is almost

1581
01:09:00,010 --> 01:09:02,937
like a single-core machine
versus a multi-core machine.

1582
01:09:02,937 --> 01:09:04,479
So when you have a
single agent, it's

1583
01:09:04,479 --> 01:09:06,670
almost like saying I have
a single processor that's

1584
01:09:06,670 --> 01:09:08,660
powering my computer.

1585
01:09:08,660 --> 01:09:10,210
But now if I have
multiple agents,

1586
01:09:10,210 --> 01:09:13,390
I have this maybe a
16-core or 64-core machine,

1587
01:09:13,390 --> 01:09:16,000
where a lot of these things can
be routed to different agents,

1588
01:09:16,000 --> 01:09:16,880
paralyzed.

1589
01:09:16,880 --> 01:09:19,750
And I think that that's a
very interesting analogy when

1590
01:09:19,750 --> 01:09:22,124
it comes to a lot of
this multi-agent systems.

1591
01:09:22,124 --> 01:09:24,870

1592
01:09:24,870 --> 01:09:27,439
There's still a lot of
work that needs to be done.

1593
01:09:27,439 --> 01:09:30,300
The biggest one is just
communication is really hard.

1594
01:09:30,300 --> 01:09:32,910
So you need robust
communication protocols

1595
01:09:32,910 --> 01:09:35,500
to minimize miscommunication.

1596
01:09:35,500 --> 01:09:37,770
You might also just need
really good schemas.

1597
01:09:37,770 --> 01:09:41,685
And maybe almost how
you have HTTP that's

1598
01:09:41,685 --> 01:09:43,810
used to transporting
information over the internet.

1599
01:09:43,810 --> 01:09:46,979
You might need something
similar to transport information

1600
01:09:46,979 --> 01:09:49,933
between different agents.

1601
01:09:49,933 --> 01:09:51,850
You can also think about
some primitives here.

1602
01:09:51,850 --> 01:09:55,020
I will just walk through a
small example if we have time.

1603
01:09:55,020 --> 01:09:58,600
Suppose this is a manager agent
that wants to get tasks done,

1604
01:09:58,600 --> 01:10:01,320
so it gives a plan and a
context to a worker agent.

1605
01:10:01,320 --> 01:10:03,760
The worker can say like,
OK, I did this task.

1606
01:10:03,760 --> 01:10:05,850
And then we get a response back.

1607
01:10:05,850 --> 01:10:07,650
Then usually you want
to actually verify

1608
01:10:07,650 --> 01:10:08,650
if this got done or not.

1609
01:10:08,650 --> 01:10:12,000
Because it's possible maybe
the worker was lying to you,

1610
01:10:12,000 --> 01:10:14,130
for example, maybe it
failed to do the task

1611
01:10:14,130 --> 01:10:15,620
or something went wrong.

1612
01:10:15,620 --> 01:10:18,120
And so you want to actually go
and verify that this actually

1613
01:10:18,120 --> 01:10:19,210
was done properly.

1614
01:10:19,210 --> 01:10:21,490
And if everything was done,
then OK, this is good.

1615
01:10:21,490 --> 01:10:23,740
You can tell the user this
task was actually finished.

1616
01:10:23,740 --> 01:10:26,423
But what could happen is
maybe the worker actually

1617
01:10:26,423 --> 01:10:27,590
didn't do the task properly.

1618
01:10:27,590 --> 01:10:28,580
Something went wrong.

1619
01:10:28,580 --> 01:10:31,600
And in that case, you want to
actually go and redo the task.

1620
01:10:31,600 --> 01:10:34,540
And you just have to build this
sort of a failover mechanisms.

1621
01:10:34,540 --> 01:10:37,400
Because otherwise, there's a
lot of things that can go wrong.

1622
01:10:37,400 --> 01:10:40,240
And so thinking about
this syncing primitives

1623
01:10:40,240 --> 01:10:41,950
and how can you
ensure reliability,

1624
01:10:41,950 --> 01:10:43,360
how can you ensure fallback.

1625
01:10:43,360 --> 01:10:45,527
I think that becomes very
interesting with this kind

1626
01:10:45,527 --> 01:10:47,780
of agent systems.

1627
01:10:47,780 --> 01:10:51,530
And there's a lot of future
directions to be explored.

1628
01:10:51,530 --> 01:10:54,600
There's a lot of issues with
autonomous agents still.

1629
01:10:54,600 --> 01:10:56,990
The biggest ones are
around reliability.

1630
01:10:56,990 --> 01:10:59,730
So this happens because models
are stochastic in nature.

1631
01:10:59,730 --> 01:11:03,020
Like if our AI model,
it's stochastic.

1632
01:11:03,020 --> 01:11:04,700
It's a probabilistic function.

1633
01:11:04,700 --> 01:11:06,120
It's not fully deterministic.

1634
01:11:06,120 --> 01:11:09,230
And what happens is like,
if I wanted to do something,

1635
01:11:09,230 --> 01:11:12,020
it's possible that with
some error rate [INAUDIBLE],,

1636
01:11:12,020 --> 01:11:14,390
it will do something that
I didn't expect it to do.

1637
01:11:14,390 --> 01:11:16,520
And so like it becomes
really hard to trust it.

1638
01:11:16,520 --> 01:11:18,712
Because if I have traditional
code, I write a script

1639
01:11:18,712 --> 01:11:21,170
and I run the script through
a bunch of test cases and unit

1640
01:11:21,170 --> 01:11:21,450
tests.

1641
01:11:21,450 --> 01:11:24,000
I know, OK, if this works,
it's going to 100% of the time.

1642
01:11:24,000 --> 01:11:26,167
I can deploy this to millions
or billions of people.

1643
01:11:26,167 --> 01:11:28,490
But if our agent, it's
a stochastic function.

1644
01:11:28,490 --> 01:11:30,950
Maybe if it works, maybe it
works like 95% of the time.

1645
01:11:30,950 --> 01:11:33,020
But 5% of the time,
it still fails.

1646
01:11:33,020 --> 01:11:35,305
And there's no way to
fix this right now.

1647
01:11:35,305 --> 01:11:36,680
So that becomes
very interesting,

1648
01:11:36,680 --> 01:11:39,820
like, how do you actually
solve this problems?

1649
01:11:39,820 --> 01:11:42,320
Similarly, you see a lot of
problems around looping and plan

1650
01:11:42,320 --> 01:11:43,260
divergence.

1651
01:11:43,260 --> 01:11:47,510
So what happens here is you need
a lot of multi-turn interactions

1652
01:11:47,510 --> 01:11:48,750
when it comes to agents.

1653
01:11:48,750 --> 01:11:50,780
So you want to have the
agent do something, then

1654
01:11:50,780 --> 01:11:52,470
use that to do another
thing, and so on.

1655
01:11:52,470 --> 01:11:54,600
So take maybe hundreds
or thousands of steps.

1656
01:11:54,600 --> 01:11:57,530
But if it fails in
maybe the 20th step,

1657
01:11:57,530 --> 01:11:59,450
then it might just go haywire.

1658
01:11:59,450 --> 01:12:02,210
I don't know what to do
for the remaining thousands

1659
01:12:02,210 --> 01:12:04,310
steps of its trajectory.

1660
01:12:04,310 --> 01:12:07,200
And so how do you correct
it, bring it back on course?

1661
01:12:07,200 --> 01:12:09,140
I think that becomes
an interesting problem.

1662
01:12:09,140 --> 01:12:11,090
Similarly, how do you test
and benchmark these agents,

1663
01:12:11,090 --> 01:12:13,590
especially if they're going to
be running in the real world?

1664
01:12:13,590 --> 01:12:15,410
And how do you build
a lot of observability

1665
01:12:15,410 --> 01:12:16,202
around the systems?

1666
01:12:16,202 --> 01:12:19,460
If I have this agent
that's maybe has access

1667
01:12:19,460 --> 01:12:21,590
to my bank account, is
doing things for me,

1668
01:12:21,590 --> 01:12:24,020
how do I actually know
it's doing safe things?

1669
01:12:24,020 --> 01:12:26,240
Someone it's not
hacking into it?

1670
01:12:26,240 --> 01:12:27,680
How do I build trust?

1671
01:12:27,680 --> 01:12:30,360
And also how do we
build human fallbacks?

1672
01:12:30,360 --> 01:12:33,440
Like you probably want something
a 2FA if it's going to go and do

1673
01:12:33,440 --> 01:12:34,190
purchases for you.

1674
01:12:34,190 --> 01:12:37,820
Or you want like some ways to
guarantee like, OK, you just

1675
01:12:37,820 --> 01:12:42,490
didn't wake up and didn't have
a $0 bank account or something.

1676
01:12:42,490 --> 01:12:45,060

1677
01:12:45,060 --> 01:12:46,740
And so these are
some of the problems

1678
01:12:46,740 --> 01:12:48,480
that we need to solve
for agents for them

1679
01:12:48,480 --> 01:12:50,700
to become like real
world deployable.

1680
01:12:50,700 --> 01:12:53,310
And this is an example
of plan divergence

1681
01:12:53,310 --> 01:12:54,730
problem you see with agents.

1682
01:12:54,730 --> 01:12:57,000
Like, if you ask the
agent to go do something,

1683
01:12:57,000 --> 01:12:59,910
you will usually
expect it to follow

1684
01:12:59,910 --> 01:13:02,550
a path, where it will follow
some ideal path to reach

1685
01:13:02,550 --> 01:13:03,160
the goal.

1686
01:13:03,160 --> 01:13:04,560
But what might happen is
like it might actually

1687
01:13:04,560 --> 01:13:05,578
deviate from the path.

1688
01:13:05,578 --> 01:13:07,620
And once it deviates, it
doesn't know what to do.

1689
01:13:07,620 --> 01:13:09,930
It just keeps making
mistakes after mistakes.

1690
01:13:09,930 --> 01:13:12,330
And this is something
actually you

1691
01:13:12,330 --> 01:13:15,697
observe with early agents,
like AutoGPT, for example.

1692
01:13:15,697 --> 01:13:17,280
So I'm not sure if
anyone in this room

1693
01:13:17,280 --> 01:13:19,230
has played with AutoGPT.

1694
01:13:19,230 --> 01:13:20,120
Has anyone did that?

1695
01:13:20,120 --> 01:13:20,620
OK.

1696
01:13:20,620 --> 01:13:25,200
And so AutoGPT, it's
a very good prototype,

1697
01:13:25,200 --> 01:13:26,822
but it doesn't
actually do anything.

1698
01:13:26,822 --> 01:13:28,780
Because it just keeps
making a lot of mistakes,

1699
01:13:28,780 --> 01:13:30,655
it keeps looping around,
keeps going haywire.

1700
01:13:30,655 --> 01:13:33,980
And that's show's
like, OK, you really

1701
01:13:33,980 --> 01:13:36,480
need to have a really good ways
to correcting agents, making

1702
01:13:36,480 --> 01:13:39,720
sure if it makes a mistake, it
can actually come back and not

1703
01:13:39,720 --> 01:13:41,180
just go do random things.

1704
01:13:41,180 --> 01:13:44,750

1705
01:13:44,750 --> 01:13:48,350
So building on this, there's
also a very good analogy

1706
01:13:48,350 --> 01:13:49,700
from Andrej Karpathy.

1707
01:13:49,700 --> 01:13:52,565
So he likes to call this
the LLM operating system.

1708
01:13:52,565 --> 01:13:56,570

1709
01:13:56,570 --> 01:14:00,080
I was talking about thing
about LLMs and the agents

1710
01:14:00,080 --> 01:14:02,880
as building computer
chips and computers.

1711
01:14:02,880 --> 01:14:05,420
So you can actually start
thinking of it like that.

1712
01:14:05,420 --> 01:14:07,550
Here the computer
chip is the LLM

1713
01:14:07,550 --> 01:14:11,690
and the RAM is the context
length of the tokens

1714
01:14:11,690 --> 01:14:13,220
that you're feeding
into the model.

1715
01:14:13,220 --> 01:14:14,762
Then you have this
file system, which

1716
01:14:14,762 --> 01:14:17,373
is a disk, where you are
storing your embeddings.

1717
01:14:17,373 --> 01:14:19,040
You're able to retrieve
this embeddings.

1718
01:14:19,040 --> 01:14:21,320
You might have traditional
software, 1.0 tools,

1719
01:14:21,320 --> 01:14:24,980
which are like your calculator,
your Python interpreter,

1720
01:14:24,980 --> 01:14:26,302
terminal, et cetera.

1721
01:14:26,302 --> 01:14:27,260
You actually have this.

1722
01:14:27,260 --> 01:14:29,925
If you have an
operating system course,

1723
01:14:29,925 --> 01:14:31,550
there's this thing
called an ALU, which

1724
01:14:31,550 --> 01:14:33,567
is an arithmetic
logical unit, which

1725
01:14:33,567 --> 01:14:35,900
powers a lot of how you do
multiplications, division, so

1726
01:14:35,900 --> 01:14:36,330
on.

1727
01:14:36,330 --> 01:14:37,538
So it's very similar to that.

1728
01:14:37,538 --> 01:14:41,900
You just need tools to be
able to do complex operations.

1729
01:14:41,900 --> 01:14:45,185
And then you might also
have peripheral devices.

1730
01:14:45,185 --> 01:14:46,810
So you might have
different modalities,

1731
01:14:46,810 --> 01:14:48,990
so you might have audio,
you might have video.

1732
01:14:48,990 --> 01:14:51,700
You probably want to be able
to connect to the internet,

1733
01:14:51,700 --> 01:14:54,130
so you want to have some sort
of browsing capabilities.

1734
01:14:54,130 --> 01:14:57,180
And you might also want to be
able to talk to other LLMs.

1735
01:14:57,180 --> 01:14:58,893
And so this kind
of becomes how you

1736
01:14:58,893 --> 01:15:00,810
will think about a new
generation of computers

1737
01:15:00,810 --> 01:15:03,090
being designed with all
the innovations in AI.

1738
01:15:03,090 --> 01:15:06,140

1739
01:15:06,140 --> 01:15:07,220
Cool.

1740
01:15:07,220 --> 01:15:11,030
And so I'd like to end here
by saying how I imagine this

1741
01:15:11,030 --> 01:15:12,290
to look like in the future.

1742
01:15:12,290 --> 01:15:15,440
You can think of this as a
neural computer, where there's

1743
01:15:15,440 --> 01:15:18,710
a user that's talking to a chat
interface, behind the scene,

1744
01:15:18,710 --> 01:15:20,870
the chat interface
has an action engine

1745
01:15:20,870 --> 01:15:24,020
that can take the task route
it to different agents,

1746
01:15:24,020 --> 01:15:28,510
and do the task for you, and
send you the results back.

1747
01:15:28,510 --> 01:15:29,010
Cool.

1748
01:15:29,010 --> 01:15:33,490

1749
01:15:33,490 --> 01:15:37,613
Yeah, so to end this, a
lot of stuff that still

1750
01:15:37,613 --> 01:15:38,780
needs to be done for agents.

1751
01:15:38,780 --> 01:15:41,860
And the biggest prevalent
issues right now, I will say

1752
01:15:41,860 --> 01:15:42,743
is error correction.

1753
01:15:42,743 --> 01:15:44,660
So like, what happens
if something goes wrong?

1754
01:15:44,660 --> 01:15:45,910
How do you prevent the
errors from activating

1755
01:15:45,910 --> 01:15:46,990
in the real world?

1756
01:15:46,990 --> 01:15:48,320
How do you build security?

1757
01:15:48,320 --> 01:15:50,140
How do you build
user permissions?

1758
01:15:50,140 --> 01:15:53,270
What if someone tries to hijack
your computer or your agent?

1759
01:15:53,270 --> 01:15:57,400
How do you build robust
like a security primitives?

1760
01:15:57,400 --> 01:15:59,440
And also, how do you
sandbox these agents?

1761
01:15:59,440 --> 01:16:01,190
How do you deploy them
in risky scenarios?

1762
01:16:01,190 --> 01:16:02,857
So if you want to
deploy this in finance

1763
01:16:02,857 --> 01:16:04,700
scenarios or legal scenarios.

1764
01:16:04,700 --> 01:16:06,700
There's a lot of things
where you just want this

1765
01:16:06,700 --> 01:16:09,310
to be very stable and safe.

1766
01:16:09,310 --> 01:16:11,990
And that's like still something
that has not been figured out.

1767
01:16:11,990 --> 01:16:16,510
And there's a lot of exciting
room to think on these problems,

1768
01:16:16,510 --> 01:16:20,570
both on the research side
as well as application side.

1769
